window.__CS249R_CHAPTERS__["vol2/03_network_fabrics"] = {"key": "vol2/03_network_fabrics", "sections": [{"id": "sec-1", "level": 2, "title": "Purpose"}, {"id": "sec-2", "level": 2, "title": "The Synchronization Backbone"}, {"id": "sec-3", "level": 2, "title": "How ML Networking Inverts Data-Center Assumptions"}, {"id": "sec-4", "level": 3, "title": "The five-level model"}, {"id": "sec-5", "level": 2, "title": "Level 1 Wire and Link"}, {"id": "sec-6", "level": 3, "title": "Signal integrity and PAM4"}, {"id": "sec-7", "level": 3, "title": "Reach and medium for copper and optics"}, {"id": "sec-8", "level": 3, "title": "SerDes, link budget, and power"}, {"id": "sec-9", "level": 2, "title": "Level 2 Transport and the Performance Model"}, {"id": "sec-10", "level": 3, "title": "RDMA and GPUDirect"}, {"id": "sec-11", "level": 3, "title": "InfiniBand and RoCE"}, {"id": "sec-12", "level": 3, "title": "Reliable delivery and loss recovery"}, {"id": "sec-13", "level": 3, "title": "The $\\MessageStartupLatency$-$\\LinkBandwidth$ performance model"}, {"id": "sec-14", "level": 2, "title": "Level 3 Switch and Topology"}, {"id": "sec-15", "level": 3, "title": "Top-of-rack (ToR) and the failure domain"}, {"id": "sec-16", "level": 3, "title": "Fat-tree (Clos) networks"}, {"id": "sec-17", "level": 3, "title": "Rail-optimized topology"}, {"id": "sec-18", "level": 3, "title": "Dragonfly and torus alternatives"}, {"id": "sec-19", "level": 3, "title": "Optical circuit switching and reconfigurable topology"}, {"id": "sec-20", "level": 2, "title": "Level 4 Fabric Behavior, Congestion, and Routing"}, {"id": "sec-21", "level": 3, "title": "Priority flow control (PFC)"}, {"id": "sec-22", "level": 3, "title": "Lossless fabric hazards and watchdog recovery"}, {"id": "sec-23", "level": 3, "title": "End-to-end congestion control with DCQCN and HPCC"}, {"id": "sec-24", "level": 3, "title": "Adaptive routing"}, {"id": "sec-25", "level": 3, "title": "The incast problem in ML"}, {"id": "sec-26", "level": 2, "title": "Level 5 Cluster Design and Case Studies"}, {"id": "sec-27", "level": 3, "title": "The GPU-to-GPU \"gradient bus\""}, {"id": "sec-28", "level": 3, "title": "NVIDIA DGX SuperPOD architecture"}, {"id": "sec-29", "level": 3, "title": "Meta Grand Teton platform"}, {"id": "sec-30", "level": 2, "title": "Network Virtualization"}, {"id": "sec-31", "level": 3, "title": "Hardware NIC virtualization with SR-IOV"}, {"id": "sec-32", "level": 3, "title": "Traffic isolation and quality of service"}, {"id": "sec-33", "level": 2, "title": "Monitoring and Debugging"}, {"id": "sec-34", "level": 3, "title": "Link-level telemetry"}, {"id": "sec-35", "level": 3, "title": "Bandwidth and latency validation"}, {"id": "sec-36", "level": 3, "title": "Systematic debugging workflow"}, {"id": "sec-37", "level": 2, "title": "Network Technologies That Move the Ceiling"}, {"id": "sec-38", "level": 3, "title": "Protocol convergence and link density"}, {"id": "sec-39", "level": 3, "title": "Optical interconnects"}, {"id": "sec-40", "level": 3, "title": "Disaggregated and composable architectures"}], "html": "<h1 id=\"sec-1\" class=\"cs-h1\">Network Fabrics</h1>\n<h2 id=\"sec-1\" class=\"cs-h2\">Purpose</h2>\n<p><em>Why can the network connecting accelerators matter more than the accelerators themselves at scale?</em></p>\n<p>A single accelerator can perform trillions of operations per second, but distributed training requires devices to exchange results before dependent work can proceed. When network capacity cannot keep pace with computation, accelerators sit idle, and adding more devices can increase the waiting instead of shortening the run. The resulting cost extends beyond the network equipment: an inexpensive connection can leave expensive compute capacity unused throughout a long experiment. Single-node intuition is insufficient because data that once traveled through a local interconnect must now cross cables, switches, and shared paths. A fast connection between two machines does not establish that the same performance will hold when the entire fleet communicates at once. The arrangement of those paths determines which groups can exchange data together and where competing transfers accumulate. Large transfers need sustained bandwidth, while frequent dependent exchanges can remain limited by the time required to start and complete each message. A delayed participant can hold up others even when most links appear healthy. Network design therefore has to follow the workload's communication pattern, and validation must reflect the traffic that will actually run. Choices about placement, sharing, and failure recovery affect whether available bandwidth becomes useful progress or recurring delay. In C³ terms, the fabric connects compute through communication, while coordination determines when the fleet must wait for that communication to finish.</p>\n<div class=\"cs-callout\"><div class=\"cs-callout-title\">Learning Objectives</div>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Explain how synchronized collective traffic inverts data-center assumptions about flows, loss, and oversubscription</li>\n<li>Model network communication cost using the $\\MessageStartupLatency$-$\\LinkBandwidth$ framework and identify bandwidth-dominated vs. latency-dominated regimes</li>\n<li>Compare high-performance transport protocols by latency, loss-recovery behavior, and operational complexity</li>\n<li>Compare network topologies using $\\BisectionBandwidth$, hop count, and the workload's collective communication pattern</li>\n<li>Evaluate congestion-control mechanisms and lossless-fabric hazards by their impact on tail latency during distributed training</li>\n<li>Compare network partitioning and traffic-isolation mechanisms for checkpoint and multi-tenant traffic</li>\n<li>Diagnose network performance bottlenecks using RDMA counters, link-level telemetry, and bandwidth testing tools</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"sec-2\" class=\"cs-h2\">The Synchronization Backbone</h2>\n<p>At the node boundary, each GPU's NVLink bandwidth narrows to a single external network port, the bottom of the bandwidth hierarchy traced in @sec-compute-infrastructure. How much that narrowing costs depends on what the training job must push through the port, and how often. Consider the running instance of Archetype A (GPT-4/Llama-3), a <code>[NetworkRunningExampleCluster.params_str]</code>-parameter GPT-3-class dense transformer partitioned across the <code>[NetworkRunningExampleCluster.n_gpus_str]</code> of the reference cluster. Each training step must combine gradient contributions so that corresponding model replicas apply the same update. This operation, called AllReduce, returns the combined result to every participating worker.</p>\n<p>The model has <code>[NetworkRunningExampleCluster.grad_gb_str]</code> of gradient data at two bytes per parameter, the 16-bit gradients of mixed-precision training that this chapter assumes throughout (FP32 gradients would double the figure). That model-wide figure is not what any one GPU sends. When a reduction partitions the gradient across all <code>[NetworkRunningExampleCluster.n_gpus_value_str]</code> ranks (the numbered workers of the job), each rank owns a 1/<code>[NetworkRunningExampleCluster.n_gpus_value_str]</code> share, a few hundred megabytes, which the transport examples below round to one assumed gradient-shard size; the job's tensor, pipeline, and data-parallel split (@sec-distributed-training-systems) sets each GPU's actual payload. Worked examples that switch to another model, precision, or payload state their parameters explicitly.</p>\n<p>Because every rank participates in that shard exchange, a delayed transfer on the step's critical path can leave the other <code>[NetworkRunningExampleCluster.peer_gpus_str]</code> GPUs waiting at a job-wide synchronization boundary. The fabric's contribution to training time therefore depends on which transfers the next computation needs and how much of their cost remains exposed. In that role the fabric is not auxiliary infrastructure; it is the synchronization backbone that decides whether the cluster trains as one machine.</p>\n<p>Within the multi-tier fleet hierarchy framed by @fig-fleet-stack, the fabric turns isolated accelerator nodes into a coherent training system. Accelerators, power delivery, cooling, racks, and pods define what each node can compute in isolation; the fabric determines whether the nodes can act together before communication cost overwhelms computation. That design space organizes into a <strong>five-level model</strong> of co-dependent tiers, developed in @sec-network-fabrics-five-level-model.</p>\n<p>Why those tiers must be co-designed becomes clear at scale, where communication cost can dominate computation cost. The fleet-law decomposition in @sec-vol2-introduction-fleet-law makes this explicit: the nonoverlapped communication term $\\CommunicationTime(\\DeviceCount) - \\OverlappedTime$ determines how much of each training step is exposed to the network fabric. The fabric influences the layers above it by determining whether communication can run while independent computation continues, which collective algorithms are viable, and how network partitions interact with node failures. That concurrency, communication-computation overlap, is what the $\\OverlappedTime$ term credits, because transfer time that proceeds behind useful arithmetic does not lengthen the step.</p>\n<p>↪ How layer-by-layer scheduling achieves that overlap, and how much communication it still leaves exposed, is developed in @sec-communication-overlap.</p>\n<p>The transfers that contend with or hide behind that arithmetic are organized as collectives, where a collective is a communication operation performed by a group of workers. Its name specifies the result each worker receives, while an algorithm determines the messages that produce that result. The physical network fabric carries three recurring collective communication patterns:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>AllReduce</strong>: Combines corresponding values from all participants and returns the same result to each; synchronous training uses a sum, followed by normalization, to average gradient contributions across workers.</li>\n<li><strong>AllGather</strong>: Collects a distinct portion from each participant and returns the concatenated collection to every participant, as required when reconstructing a sharded parameter tensor.</li>\n<li><strong>AllToAll</strong>: Lets each participant send a distinct portion to every other participant, an exchange central to expert parallelism when workers host different expert subnetworks and must route tokens to their assigned destinations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Collective communication algorithms orchestrate these patterns, while the fabric layer supplies the physical wires and switches that carry them. The distinction matters because the fabric's physical properties (bandwidth, latency, and topology) determine which patterns are efficient and which become bottlenecks.</p>\n<p>↪ The full set of collective primitives, with the output contract each one guarantees, is developed in @sec-collective-communication-six-core-primitives-8a66.</p>\n<p>In a single machine, the memory bus moves data between the processor and memory. In a distributed training cluster, the network fabric serves an analogous role as the gradient bus that carries contributions between workers. When those exchanges limit progress, the job encounters the network wall mapped across the pod tier in @fig-three-walls-grid. Protocols, topologies, congestion control, and collective schedules determine how effectively the job uses the available paths. This bus has a narrow segment. In the reported product chronology, the external-port budgets remain below the aggregate NVLink budget from its first plotted generation onward (@fig-network-bandwidth-scaling). Product years mix confirmed shipments with announcements and planned availability; they do not establish an exact first-shipment chronology. How much of that gap a job actually feels depends on its message sizes, port provisioning, routing, and contention, so the link ratio bounds the problem rather than predicting a transfer time.</p>\n<p>↩ Communication dominance at scale, the network wall, is established in @sec-introduction-communication-becomes-dominant-e1b6.</p>\n<p>The reference hardware gives the gradient bus analogy a concrete scale. A single H100 has a precision-specific FP16 peak of <code>[NetworkFabricsContextScenario.h100_fp16_tflop_per_s_str]</code> and memory bandwidth of <code>[NetworkFabricsContextScenario.h100_mem_bw_tb_per_s_str]</code>. Each GPU's NVLink ports together provide <code>[NetworkFabricsContextScenario.nvlink_h100_gb_per_s_str]</code> of bidirectional bandwidth to its peers within the node. The comparison that matters is per GPU and per direction. In the reference node each GPU has its own network interface card (NIC) with one NDR InfiniBand port, so a tensor leaving the node through that GPU drops from <code>[NetworkFabricsContextScenario.nvlink_h100_perdir_gb_per_s_str]</code> of NVLink bandwidth to <code>[NetworkFabricsContextScenario.ib_ndr_gb_per_s_str]</code> at the port. This <code>[NetworkFabricsContextScenario.nvlink_to_ib_ratio_mult_str]</code> gap is the node boundary's cliff that the bandwidth hierarchy of @sec-compute-bandwidth-hierarchy already identified. The model-wide <code>[NetworkRunningExampleCluster.grad_gb_str]</code> gradient state does not all pass through every <code>[NetworkFabricsContextScenario.ib_ndr_gb_per_s_str]</code> port; partitioning and the collective schedule determine each port's traffic. Converting that gap into a step-time estimate requires the workload's local payloads and communication dependencies.</p>\n<p>↪ The network hierarchy ratio between intra-node and inter-node bandwidth, with the reference NVLink and InfiniBand values behind it, is developed in @sec-appdx-fleet-foundations-numbers-to-know.</p>\n<p>Which traffic must cross that gap depends on how the job is partitioned. The same hierarchy keeps tensor parallelism, with its per-layer exchanges, inside the NVLink domain and lets pipeline stages and data-parallel replicas span nodes, the combination called 3D parallelism whose schedules are developed in @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-hybrid-parallelism-3d-loop. The job has one further lever over that traffic. Each data-parallel replica can sum gradients over several microbatches, the small slices of a batch, before an exchange (gradient accumulation), reducing how often it must cross the node boundary at the cost of changing the batch and update schedule.</p>\n<p>When traffic does cross the node boundary, any exposed communication leaves gaps between dependent compute phases. Two assumed training schedules on a common 230 ms axis, each containing 100 ms forward and backward compute phases, make that cost visible (@fig-bandwidth-cliff-gantt). The 8-GPU intra-node case assumes a 1 ms AllReduce and spends 99.5 percent of the step in those compute phases; the 64-GPU inter-node case assumes a 30 ms AllReduce and spends 87 percent in computation. The two AllReduce durations are illustrative inputs chosen to show the effect, not values derived from the link rates above, and the percentages are shares of the schedule rather than measured arithmetic utilization.</p>\n<p>In Fleet Law terms, the widened red block is the part of $\\CommunicationTime(\\DeviceCount)$ that no $\\OverlappedTime$ has hidden. In the inter-node schedule it is 30 ms of every 230 ms step, about 13 percent, and that share applies to every accelerator-hour the cluster spends for the life of the run. Network fabric design therefore belongs at the center of distributed-training design rather than at its edge, and keeping that exposed window small requires rethinking the traffic assumptions that govern conventional data-center fabrics.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h2\">How ML Networking Inverts Data-Center Assumptions</h2>\n<p>A conventional cloud network built for millions of independent web requests breaks down when a training job releases its gradient exchanges in lockstep at the end of a backward pass. Statistical multiplexing relies on traffic peaks not all coinciding, whereas synchronized workers demand the same switch paths at the same microsecond. Web services also have dependency chains and tail-latency requirements, but a bulk-synchronous training job ties every participating worker to a single shared completion boundary.</p>\n<p>The ML networking inversion in @tbl-network-assumptions concerns this change in traffic dependencies. Synchronous collective phases can generate correlated bursts and long-lived flows that must complete before the next computation can start. Fabric validation must therefore measure collective completion under the expected load; average per-flow throughput alone does not establish that the job can meet its step-time budget.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Workload pattern</strong></th><th><strong>Traditional data-center assumption</strong></th><th><strong>ML reality</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Traffic pattern</strong></td><td>Independent requests can arrive asynchronously</td><td>Collective phases can release correlated bursts</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Flow type</strong></td><td>Many short transfers can share available paths</td><td>Large collective transfers can create persistent \"elephant\" flows</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Performance metric</strong></td><td>Per-request completion and throughput</td><td>Collective completion and exposed step time</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Loss tolerance</strong></td><td>Recovery delays affected requests</td><td>Recovery can delay dependent workers at a barrier</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Congestion</strong></td><td>Statistical multiplexing relies on noncoincident peaks</td><td>Shared paths and fan-in can concentrate synchronized traffic</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>ML Networking Inverts Traditional Data-Center Assumptions</strong>: The comparison contrasts independent-request traffic with synchronized collective phases. Correlated demand and shared completion boundaries change the fabric's capacity and latency requirements. {#tbl-network-assumptions}</p>\n<p>The bulk synchronous parallel (BSP) model, the lockstep at step barriers already met at the scale of one node (@sec-compute-node), makes the dependency in @tbl-network-assumptions explicit. In the synchronous training example, all <code>[NetworkRunningExampleCluster.n_gpus_str]</code> must complete the required gradient exchange before the next step can proceed. A 100 ms transfer delay adds 100 ms to that step only if it extends the critical path by the full interval; other computation or communication may hide part of it. At a barrier that waits for the last required result, <strong>tail latency</strong>, the slow end of the completion-time distribution (commonly reported as its 99th percentile, p99), decides when the step ends, so the tail cannot be dismissed as an outlier.</p>\n<p>: [offset=-14mm] <strong>BSP (bulk synchronous parallel)</strong>: Proposed by @valiant1990 as a bridging model between parallel hardware and software. Synchronous data-parallel training follows the same barrier structure [@goyal2017accurate; @li2020pytorch].</p>\n<p><em><strong>Bulk synchronous parallel (BSP)</strong></em> is a parallel execution model in which every worker completes a local computation phase, exchanges the data required by the next phase, and then waits at a global barrier before any worker begins the next phase, which makes the slowest participant the pacing constraint for the entire cluster.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: The slowest required computation or communication determines when a superstep can complete. Faster workers wait at the barrier, so a localized delay can consume idle accelerator time across the entire participating group.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: BSP organizes execution into synchronized supersteps; it does not itself prescribe a learning algorithm or a particular exchange pattern. Synchronous data-parallel updates match a single-device reference only under compatible global batches, update rules, and numerical assumptions.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: Removing barriers does not by itself establish faster time-to-quality. Asynchronous training trades waiting against stale updates, and the convergence and wall-clock comparison depends on the workload and update schedule.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>↩ Synchronous tight coupling, the shared step barrier that lets the slowest worker pace a training fleet, is established in @sec-introduction-traditional-vs-ml-fleet-dynamics-c43e.</p>\n<p>The flow inversion compounds this problem. Traditional networks are designed for fairness among millions of \"mice flows,\" yet ML training is dominated by a few \"elephant flows\" corresponding to the gradient AllReduce. The full 175B model has <code>[NetworkRunningExampleCluster.grad_gb_str]</code> of gradient state at the assumed precision, but a sharded job distributes that state across workers. Each collective operates on its participating group and local payload, and its algorithm determines how much each worker sends. Standard routing such as equal-cost multi-path (ECMP)}}, which hashes each flow onto one of several equal-cost paths, is poorly suited to this traffic pattern. A static hash cannot subdivide one elephant flow across all available links, and it can inadvertently map multiple elephant flows to the same link, creating a hot spot while other links sit idle.</p>\n<p>: [offset=2mm] <strong>Elephant and mice flows</strong>: The model-wide gradient size is not the size of one network flow. A collective can split each worker's local gradient shard across channels and transport connections; the resulting long-lived flows can still collide under ECMP when their static hashes select the same path.</p>\n<p>The loss inversion completes the picture. TCP was designed to recover across an unreliable network, and an occasional retransmission delays only the request it belongs to. In a synchronous collective, a lost packet delays the transfer that every waiting worker needs, and some NICs that implement remote direct memory access (RDMA), called RDMA NICs or RNICs, recover by resending everything after the missing packet rather than only the lost region (Go-Back-N-style recovery). ML fabrics therefore aim to make loss rare and recovery bounded, either by preventing buffer overflow at the link layer or by building transports that recover quickly from it. Level 2 (@sec-network-fabrics-losslessness) develops both approaches.</p>\n<p>Correlated demand also makes <strong>oversubscription</strong>, provisioning less uplink capacity at a switch tier than the downlink capacity it serves (written as a downlink:uplink ratio such as 2:1), a workload decision. A fabric with spare capacity under independent requests can become saturated when a collective releases traffic across the same cut. Designing for that traffic requires tracing the constraint from the wire through transport, topology, and congestion control to the job's synchronization boundary.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h3\">The five-level model</h3>\n<p>Provisioning fast NICs or buying a non-blocking (fully provisioned) switch tier in isolation routinely fails to speed up training: a 400 Gb/s link still stalls if host TCP copies starve the PCIe bus, an unaligned rack layout exhausts the copper link budget, or static ECMP hashing piles two elephant flows onto one port of an upper-tier spine switch. Tracing why a collective slows down requires following the packet across five coupled layers, from serializer/deserializer (SerDes) signaling and copper or optical reach at Level 1 to the integration of a production cluster at Level 5 (@fig-network-five-level-model). Each level is developed at the point where its physical or protocol limit dominates.</p>\n<p>Each inversion of @tbl-network-assumptions traces back to a specific physical or protocol constraint, and the five levels locate where each one is paid:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Level 1: wire and link</strong> (@sec-network-fabrics-wire-link). Signal integrity (PAM4, SerDes), forward error correction, and the reach of copper and optics impose hard constraints on per-hop latency and cluster geometry.</li>\n<li><strong>Level 2: transport</strong> (@sec-network-fabrics-transport). InfiniBand and RDMA over Converged Ethernet (RoCE) provide RDMA primitives; the $\\MessageStartupLatency$-$\\LinkBandwidth$ model quantifies the latency-vs.-bandwidth trade-off for different message sizes.</li>\n<li><strong>Level 3: switch and topology</strong> (@sec-network-fabrics-topology). Fat-trees, rail-optimized designs, and dragonflies achieve the $\\BisectionBandwidth$ (bisection bandwidth, the capacity across the narrowest cut that splits the cluster in half) needed for global collectives through different structural trade-offs.</li>\n<li><strong>Level 4: fabric behavior</strong> (@sec-network-fabrics-behavior). Link-level flow control and end-to-end congestion control determine whether theoretical bandwidth translates to realized throughput; adaptive routing and incast behavior determine whether that throughput survives real collective traffic [@alizadeh2010; @zhu2015; @li2019; @gangidi2024rdma].</li>\n<li><strong>Level 5: cluster design</strong> (@sec-network-fabrics-cluster). Production supercomputers like the NVIDIA SuperPOD and Meta Grand Teton integrate these layers into a unified gradient bus [@nvidia2023dgxSuperpodH100; @meta2024genaiInfrastructure].</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The levels are not an inventory of networking topics; they are the causal chain that turns synchronous ML traffic into infrastructure requirements. Large gradient messages first stress the wire and transport, then force topology choices that preserve bisection bandwidth, then expose congestion behavior that ordinary enterprise networks can hide, and finally determine whether the whole cluster behaves like one training machine.</p>\n<p>The traffic pattern determines which fabric metric matters. A global reduction stresses bisection bandwidth and the slowest path; a point-to-point pipeline transfer sits on the critical path, so its full transfer time and hop count matter; an expert exchange stresses path diversity and egress balance; and checkpoint or tenant traffic stresses isolation from the synchronization path (@tbl-network-fabrics-traffic-metrics). The remaining levels develop each of these pairings in turn.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Traffic pattern</strong></th><th><strong>Fabric pressure</strong></th><th><strong>Metric that predicts performance</strong></th><th><strong>Primary design response</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Global reduction</td><td>Sustained traffic across a cluster cut</td><td>delivered bisection bandwidth; slowest-path completion time</td><td>non-blocking capacity, rail alignment, topology-aware collectives</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pipeline point-to-point</td><td>Critical-path activation handoffs</td><td>startup latency plus size/bandwidth; hop count</td><td>short paths, direct placement, transport offload</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Expert AllToAll</td><td>Many simultaneous worker pairs</td><td>path utilization; egress queue depth; tail latency</td><td>adaptive routing, path diversity, balanced expert placement</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Checkpoint and multi-tenant traffic</td><td>Bursts competing with training flows</td><td>per-class bandwidth; pause duration; interference</td><td>traffic classes (virtual lanes, QoS), admission control</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Traffic Patterns Determine Fabric Metrics</strong>: Workload semantics translate into different physical bottlenecks, so no single link-rate benchmark predicts training performance. The table is a map of what follows; the mechanisms in its right two columns (transport offload, adaptive routing, virtual lanes, and the rest) are developed at Levels 2 through 5. {#tbl-network-fabrics-traffic-metrics tbl-colwidths=\"[20,27,28,25]\"}</p>\n<p>Mapping workload semantics to physical constraints shows that the workload sets the requirements at every level, while each level rests on the foundation beneath it. Because no transport protocol, switch topology, or congestion controller can outrun the channel that carries its symbols, tracing the five-level stack begins at Level 1 with the physical link between two ports.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h2\">Level 1 Wire and Link</h2>\n<p>At <code>[IbNdrWireRecap.ib_ndr_gbit_per_s_str]</code> and beyond, the physics of signal transmission imposes hard limits on cable length, power consumption, and error rates. Signal attenuation in copper and chromatic dispersion in fiber dictate how far an accelerator can sit from its switches before communication fails. Every higher-level protocol and topology is ultimately constrained by what the physical wire can carry; these limits shape the geometry of the entire cluster.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h3\">Signal integrity and PAM4</h3>\n<p>Achieving <code>[IbNdrWireRecap.ib_ndr_gbit_per_s_str]</code> and higher line rates requires balancing signaling speed against channel loss and detection errors. A port reaches such a rate by summing several lanes, the parallel serial channels that each carry one signal, so a <code>[IbNdrWireRecap.ib_ndr_gbit_per_s_str]</code> NDR port carries four lanes at 100 Gb/s each. The <strong>symbol rate</strong> of a lane counts transmitted symbols per second, each occupying one time slot whether or not the voltage changes. Raising the symbol rate raises the frequencies the channel must carry, and copper loses more signal at higher frequencies. Carrying more bits in each symbol, by using more than two signal levels, raises the bit rate without that penalty, and PAM4 is the scheme high-speed links use to do it.</p>\n<p><em><strong>PAM4 signaling</strong></em>}} is a modulation scheme that represents two bits per symbol with four signal levels, doubling the raw bit rate relative to binary signaling at the same symbol rate.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: PAM4 increases the raw $\\Bandwidth$ available at a given symbol rate. The reduced separation between levels makes error-control design part of that gain. High-speed Ethernet uses forward error correction (FEC), including Reed-Solomon RS(544,514), with processing latency that depends on the physical-layer (PHY) design and implementation [@anslow2016rsFec; @yin2022fecRequirements]. FEC contributes to the fixed path budget modeled by the $\\MessageStartupLatency$ term in @sec-network-fabrics-performance-model; it is one component alongside propagation, switching, and endpoint processing.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: At equal symbol rate and voltage swing, NRZ (Non-Return-to-Zero) binary signaling carries one bit per symbol with greater separation between levels. PAM4 carries two bits per symbol with smaller level spacing; the link must compensate through its signal-quality and error-correction design.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: Doubling bits per symbol does not guarantee twice the usable payload throughput or a fixed latency penalty. Coding overhead, channel quality, and the chosen PHY implementation determine delivered performance.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The price is noise margin. With four levels packed into the same voltage swing, the gap between adjacent levels in PAM4 shrinks to one third of the NRZ gap (@fig-pam4-vs-nrz), making the link highly susceptible to noise. Consequently, high-speed links operate near the physical limits of reliable detection and require <strong>forward error correction (FEC)</strong> at the physical layer, commonly using Reed-Solomon RS(544,514)-family codes in high-speed Ethernet designs [@anslow2016rsFec; @ethernetAlliance2025roadmap].</p>\n<p>FEC processing is an implementation-dependent latency term; IEEE 802.3df operator material treats roughly 100 ns of host-side FEC with the RS(544,514) code (called KP4 in the standard) and hundreds of nanoseconds of end-to-end module latency as meaningful at AI/HPC fabric timescales [@yin2022fecRequirements]. Because each switch on a path decodes and re-encodes the signal, the FEC cost accumulates with the number of switches a packet traverses.</p>\n<p>A path's length is its <strong>hop count</strong>, counted as switch traversals or as inter-switch links; where the two counts differ, each example states which one it uses. A two-tier fabric attaches servers to leaf switches and joins the leaves through spine switches (@sec-network-fabrics-fat-tree), so a packet between leaves traverses three switches. At an assumed 100 to 200 ns per switch, that leaf-spine-leaf route incurs a scenario budget of <code>[FECLatency.three_hop_low_ns_str]</code>--<code>[FECLatency.three_hop_high_ns_str]</code> of one-way FEC latency, or <code>[FECLatency.round_trip_low_ns_str]</code>--<code>[FECLatency.round_trip_high_ns_str]</code> for a round trip.</p>\n<p>Against the <code>[NetworkRunningExampleCluster.grad_gb_str]</code> gradient of the 175B job, a few hundred nanoseconds are negligible, because bulk transfers are governed by bandwidth. The cost lands instead on small, latency-bound messages, such as the control handoffs that coordinate pipeline stages, and on collectives whose many sequential steps each pay it. This physical-layer latency sets a floor on the fabric's fixed startup latency, formalized in @sec-network-fabrics-performance-model as the $\\MessageStartupLatency$ term, that no software optimization can remove.</p>\n<p>Doubling line rate without doubling the symbol rate therefore trades signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) for bandwidth, and forward error correction is what lets the link operate that close to the channel limit. Because that tighter margin leaves less room for channel attenuation, it also shortens the distance a passive copper cable can span before the link must switch to optics.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h3\">Reach and medium for copper and optics</h3>\n<p>}}Past one to three meters, passive copper attenuates high-frequency PAM4 signals beyond what even FEC can recover, forcing any link that exits a rack onto active optical cables or pluggable optics at a steep jump in cost and power. The choice of physical medium therefore determines how far a link can reach before latency, power, and cabling budgets reshape the cluster geometry, and the recurring cable and optics options differ along exactly those attributes (@tbl-network-fabrics-link-media).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Medium</strong></th><th><strong>Reach</strong></th><th><strong>Cost profile</strong></th><th><strong>Latency impact</strong></th><th><strong>Typical placement</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>DAC (direct attach copper)</strong></td><td>1 to 3 meters, shrinking as lane rate rises</td><td>Lowest cost (~<code>[SpineOpticsCost.dac_cost_usd_str]</code>) and no optical module power</td><td>Lowest media latency; no optical conversion, though high-speed lanes still pay FEC</td><td>Within a rack</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>AOC (active optical cable)</strong></td><td>3 to 30 meters</td><td>Higher cost (~<code>[SpineOpticsCost.aoc_cost_usd_str]</code>) from permanently attached transceivers (the modules that convert between electrical and optical signals)</td><td>Electrical/optical conversion adds latency on top of the forward error correction (FEC)</td><td>Rack-to-rack or row-scale links</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Pluggable optics</strong></td><td>More than 30 meters</td><td>Highest module and power cost, but replaceable optics and fiber cords</td><td>Optical conversion adds latency on top of FEC; distance enables topology reach</td><td>Network core and pod-scale links</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Cable and Optics Placement Trade-Offs</strong>: Common interconnect media occupy different reach, latency, and cost points, so cluster designers use copper inside racks and optics as distance begins to dominate placement. {#tbl-network-fabrics-link-media tbl-colwidths=\"[18,20,20,20,22]\"}</p>\n<p>The high-speed copper and optical paths considered here both include FEC, so choosing optics does not by itself remove error-correction overhead. The ranges in @tbl-network-fabrics-link-media describe representative placements rather than fixed boundaries between media. Optical interconnects can extend the layout beyond a passive copper channel's budget, while pluggable optics also permit separate replacement of modules and fiber. Reach, delivered latency, power, and serviceability jointly determine the choice.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>\\Needspace{30\\baselineskip}</code></pre>\n<p>In an ML fleet, distance is money. Assuming two spine-layer optical links per accelerator, a cluster of <code>[SpineOpticsCost.cluster_gpus_str]</code> requires ~<code>[SpineOpticsCost.n_links_str]</code> optical links at the spine layer alone. At <code>[SpineOpticsCost.link_cost_usd_str]</code> per complete link, that represents <code>[SpineOpticsCost.total_cost_musd_str]</code> in cabling. For power, count one selected spine-side pluggable module per link at <code>[SpineOpticsCost.link_power_w_str]</code> per module, giving <code>[SpineOpticsCost.total_power_kw_str]</code> for those endpoints alone. Each optical link also has a module at its opposite end; this subtotal excludes those modules, switch electronics, and cooling, so it is not the full fabric power budget. The cost dictates cluster geometry: architects pack accelerators as densely as possible (<code>[SpineOpticsCost.rack_power_range_str]</code> per rack) to maximize cheap copper and minimize expensive optics.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h3\">SerDes, link budget, and power</h3>\n<p>Driving thousands of high-speed links across a cluster burns tens of kilowatts just to serialize bits onto the wire, a noticeable share of the accelerators' own thermal budget. Every port depends on a <strong>serializer/deserializer (SerDes)</strong>} circuit that converts parallel data from the switch application-specific integrated circuit (ASIC) into a serial stream, and its power cost accumulates across the provisioned ports. For the <code>[NetworkRunningExampleCluster.n_gpus_value_str]</code>-GPU reference cluster, assume a full-$\\BisectionBandwidth$ fabric with roughly <code>[SerDesPowerBudget.n_links_str]</code> high-speed links across its host and switch tiers. Count one selected switch-side port per link in this power subtotal. Each counted <code>[IbNdrWireRecap.ib_ndr_gbit_per_s_str]</code> port is assigned <code>[SerDesPowerBudget.port_power_w_str]</code>, more than the <code>[SpineOpticsCost.link_power_w_str]</code> pluggable-module figure in the cost-of-distance estimate because it also counts the switch-side SerDes that drives the module. The model totals <code>[SerDesPowerBudget.interconnect_kw_str]</code>, equivalent to <code>[SerDesPowerBudget.power_frac_pct_str]</code> of the accelerators' combined thermal design power. This comparison isolates the assumed power of the selected switch-side ports, excluding the opposite endpoints and the rest of the switch chassis; it does not establish total fabric power or the fraction of measured cluster energy consumed by networking.</p>\n<p>Much of that SerDes power goes into overcoming channel attenuation, and the <strong>link budget</strong> allocates the signal loss a path can tolerate while meeting its error target. Losses are counted in decibels (dB), a logarithmic power ratio in which every 10 dB is a further tenfold attenuation (3 dB is roughly a halving), so the losses of cables and connectors in series add directly. In this simplified accounting, a lane has a <code>[LinkBudgetScenario.budget_db_str]</code> allowance. A <code>[LinkBudgetScenario.cable_m_str]</code> DAC cable contributing <code>[LinkBudgetScenario.cable_loss_db_str]</code> of loss and connectors contributing <code>[LinkBudgetScenario.connector_loss_db_str]</code> leave <code>[LinkBudgetScenario.margin_db_str]</code> of margin. Higher signaling frequencies increase copper loss, so preserving reach can require more signal conditioning, a different cable, or optics. Aggregate port speed alone does not determine cable reach; lane rate, channel construction, and active components matter. The design must meet both the signal-quality target and the power budget before the transport layer can rely on the link.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h2\">Level 2 Transport and the Performance Model</h2>\n<p>Closing the physical link budget delivers raw bits between adjacent ports, but a single AllReduce across <code>[NetworkTransportClusterRecap.n_gpus_str]</code> still stalls if host software sits in the path of every transfer or if a dropped packet triggers a multi-millisecond recovery pause. Turning raw wire rate into predictable step time requires a transport layer that offloads memory-to-memory delivery and bounds loss recovery (a contract met either through a physically lossless fabric or through loss-tolerant endpoint recovery), together with a performance model that separates fixed per-message startup latency from sustained link bandwidth.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h3\">RDMA and GPUDirect</h3>\n<p>A conventional kernel TCP path charges the host for every byte it moves. Interrupt processing, protocol work, and payload copies consume CPU cycles and memory bandwidth that the training process also needs for data loading and preprocessing, and the charge grows with aggregate I/O as many streams or NICs drive a host at <code>[IbNdrTransportRecap.ib_ndr_gbit_per_s_str]</code> link rates. Modern TCP stacks reduce the charge with segmentation offload, receive-side scaling, zero-copy interfaces, and careful non-uniform memory access (NUMA) placement, so a single stream need not exhaust a server's memory bandwidth. They do not remove it, and the contention becomes harder to provision predictably as nodes add NICs.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Remote direct memory access (RDMA)</strong></em>} is a networking technology that offloads transport processing to network interface hardware, allowing access to registered remote memory without per-transfer kernel processing of the payload.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: RDMA can reduce end-to-end message latency from tens of microseconds for a kernel TCP path to low-single-digit microseconds on a tuned fabric, shrinking the fixed startup cost that every message adds to the Fleet Law's communication term $\\CommunicationTime(\\DeviceCount)$.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Unlike a conventional kernel TCP path, RDMA offloads transport processing to NIC hardware and permits user-space access to registered memory. The resulting CPU-core and memory-bandwidth savings depend on the host, NIC, I/O path, message size, and software configuration rather than on link rate alone.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: RDMA is an access and offload model, not one loss-recovery algorithm. Reliable InfiniBand and RoCE transports detect loss and retransmit [@infiniband2000spec]. Some RNIC generations use the Go-Back-N-style recovery noted with the loss inversion, which makes a drop expensive (@sec-network-fabrics-losslessness quantifies the cost), while other designs support stronger selective recovery or tolerate lossy Ethernet without the link-layer pause mechanism (priority flow control) that many RoCE deployments rely on [@mittal2018irn]. The requirement is reliable completion with a recovery tail that the synchronized workload can tolerate, not a physically lossless Ethernet fabric in every implementation.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>RDMA bypasses the kernel data path. Before any transfer, software registers a memory buffer, which pins its pages in physical memory and gives the NIC the addresses and access rights it needs. The sending NIC can then name a registered buffer on the remote machine, and the remote NIC places the data there directly, with no remote CPU copying the payload. Offloading transport logic to the NIC in this way allows applications to read and write registered remote memory without per-message kernel intervention. Each message skips the interrupts, software protocol work, and payload copies of the kernel path, which is how RDMA shrinks the fixed startup cost.</p>\n<p>For ML, GPUDirect RDMA, the path that dense nodes pair with one NIC per GPU (@sec-compute-dense-nodes), extends this principle to accelerator memory [@nvidia2026gpudirectRdma]. Within a sending node (@fig-gpudirect-data-path), GPUDirect RDMA routes an $\\PointToPointMessageSize$-byte payload (what one participant sends, not the model-wide gradient state) along the solid green peer-to-peer path through the PCIe switch straight to the RDMA NIC, bypassing host memory entirely. Without this direct memory access (DMA) bypass, the dashed gray staging path must first copy $\\PointToPointMessageSize$ bytes from GPU memory into a registered host-memory buffer and subsequently read $\\PointToPointMessageSize$ bytes out to the NIC, generating roughly $2\\PointToPointMessageSize$ bytes of redundant host-memory bus traffic. The network still transfers the payload, and the PCIe path may comprise multiple DMA transactions; the optimization removes staging copies rather than collapsing the transfer into one transaction.</p>\n<p>: <strong>GPUDirect RDMA</strong>: Introduced by NVIDIA for Kepler-class GPUs and CUDA 5.0, GPUDirect RDMA enables a direct PCIe path between GPU memory and third-party peer devices such as network interfaces [@nvidia2026gpudirectRdma]. Before GPUDirect, every gradient transfer had to stage through host memory, consuming CPU memory bandwidth that competes with data loading. Eliminating this bounce path is one reason overlapping communication with backward-pass computation is feasible at scale.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h3\">InfiniBand and RoCE</h3>\n<p>Once GPUDirect RDMA delivers the payload to the NIC without host staging, carrying that transfer across the switch fabric requires choosing between the two RDMA transport stacks that dominate GPU clusters, a reliability-versus-operations trade-off. In their usual deployments, both stacks keep switch buffers from overflowing under synchronized bursts, and they do so in different places. InfiniBand uses <strong>credit-based flow control</strong>. Each receiver advertises its free buffer space to the sender at the other end of the link in units called credits, and the sender transmits only into space that has been advertised. A buffer therefore cannot overflow; congestion appears as senders waiting for credits rather than as dropped packets. Ethernet was designed to drop packets when buffers fill, so RoCE deployments that want the same behavior add two mechanisms. Priority flow control (PFC)} lets a filling switch port send a PAUSE frame that stops its upstream neighbor for one traffic class, and explicit congestion notification (ECN)} marks packets that pass through a filling queue so that their senders slow down before a pause is needed. InfiniBand thus builds losslessness into the link layer, while RoCE assembles it from switch configuration and end-to-end congestion control (@tbl-network-fabrics-rdma-stacks); @sec-network-fabrics-pfc develops PFC's failure modes.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Stack</strong></th><th><strong>Fabric behavior</strong></th><th><strong>Operational burden</strong></th><th><strong>Typical fit</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>InfiniBand (IB)</td><td>Purpose-built HPC switched fabric [@infiniband2000spec] with credit-based flow control, so losslessness is native at the link layer</td><td>a central subnet manager computes routes, and virtual lanes (VLs), separate buffer and credit pools on one link, isolate traffic</td><td>Dedicated training clusters where predictable tail latency outweighs Ethernet ecosystem flexibility</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>RoCE (RDMA over Converged Ethernet)</strong></td><td>RoCEv2 carries RDMA semantics by encapsulating InfiniBand transport headers in UDP/IP packets</td><td>PFC-based deployments suppress loss with PFC, ECN, and routing policy [@guo2016rdma; @gangidi2024rdma]; PFC-free designs instead place more burden on end-to-end congestion control and loss recovery [@mittal2018irn]</td><td>Multi-vendor Ethernet fleets that can absorb more congestion-control tuning</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>RDMA Transport Stack Trade-Offs</strong>: InfiniBand and RoCE expose RDMA semantics through different operational contracts, one built around native losslessness and the other around Ethernet compatibility. {#tbl-network-fabrics-rdma-stacks tbl-colwidths=\"[20,30,25,25]\"}</p>\n<p>The InfiniBand} row reflects a protocol heritage that favors hardware-managed losslessness over Ethernet compatibility. Both RDMA stacks nonetheless share the same user-space bypass across the user-space, kernel, and hardware boundary (@fig-ib-roce-stack). The conventional copied TCP/IP path on the left uses two software copies across kernel buffer boundaries and runs the protocol in software. The RDMA stack on the right traces a single amber bypass arrow from the collective communication library (such as the NVIDIA Collective Communications Library, or NCCL) and the user-space <strong>verbs API</strong> straight to the RDMA NIC, which keeps the operating system off the active data path. The verbs API is the programming interface through which software posts RDMA work requests to the NIC.</p>\n<p>: [offset=0mm] <strong>InfiniBand</strong>: Formed in 1999 from the merger of two competing server I/O standards (Future I/O and NGIO), InfiniBand was originally designed to replace PCI as a general-purpose system interconnect. Its pivot to HPC networking preserved the credit-based, hardware-managed flow control that server I/O demanded, and that heritage is why InfiniBand provides native losslessness without the PFC fragility of Ethernet-based RDMA fabrics.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h3\">Reliable delivery and loss recovery</h3>\n<p>Routing the user-space verbs API directly to hardware (@fig-ib-roce-stack) decouples collective library calls from the operating system, leaving the transport hardware and fabric to arbitrate reliable delivery and packet loss. Reliable connected transports in both InfiniBand and RoCE detect loss and retransmit [@infiniband2000spec]; credits and PFC exist to make that retransmission rare [@guo2016rdma; @gangidi2024rdma]. Losslessness is therefore a deployment contract rather than a property of RDMA as a family. Designs that operate over lossy Ethernet without PFC shift more responsibility to endpoint congestion control, packet reordering, and selective recovery [@mittal2018irn].</p>\n<p>Recovery granularity therefore matters. On an RNIC with <strong>Go-Back-N</strong>-style recovery and no out-of-order retention, a lost packet invalidates every packet that arrives after it, and the sender must retransmit from the missing sequence point. The penalty is everything the sender transmitted between the lost packet and the moment it learned of the loss. That span is the resent window, and it is at most link rate times detection delay. If the receiver's negative acknowledgment (its message reporting the gap) reaches the sender after one <code>[GoBackNWindow.detect_delay_us_str]</code> round trip, a sender at <code>[GoBackNWindow.line_rate_gb_per_s_str]</code> has meanwhile sent <code>[GoBackNWindow.resent_kb_str]</code>, all of which it must send again; a drop 900 MB into a 1 GB transfer wastes that window, not the 900 MB already delivered. The transfer stalls until the resent window arrives. When detection instead waits for the sender's own retransmission timeout, the stall grows with that timeout, although the resent window stops growing once the sender reaches its cap on data sent but not yet acknowledged. Resending at line rate takes at most one more detection delay, because the window is at most link rate times that delay. Below that cap, a faster link therefore resends more bytes per drop without shortening the stall.</p>\n<p>An RNIC with selective recovery retransmits only the missing packets, so the same physical loss produces a shorter tail [@mittal2018irn]. Either way the stall spans many packet wire times, and a synchronous collective exposes the slowest recovery to every waiting worker.</p>\n<p>Fabric designers can reduce that tail by suppressing drops with PFC, by using a loss-tolerant transport with bounded endpoint recovery, or by combining congestion control and admission policy so queues rarely overflow. Each choice moves state and failure modes between switches and endpoints.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h3\">The $\\MessageStartupLatency$-$\\LinkBandwidth$ performance model</h3>\n<p>Even on a lossless RDMA path with zero packet drops, doubling the link rate barely speeds up a few-kilobyte pipeline control handoff while cutting a multi-hundred-megabyte gradient transfer nearly in half. Separating those two behaviors requires a cost model that distinguishes fixed per-message overhead from per-byte transmission rate. The $\\MessageStartupLatency$-$\\LinkBandwidth$ model (principle \\ref{pri-alpha-beta-model}) decomposes message transfer time as $\\PointToPointTransferTime(\\PointToPointMessageSize) = \\MessageStartupLatency+ \\PointToPointMessageSize/\\LinkBandwidth$, where $\\PointToPointMessageSize$ is the message size in bytes, $\\MessageStartupLatency$ is the fixed startup latency, and $\\LinkBandwidth$ is the sustained bandwidth in bytes per unit time [@hockney1994]. The physical topology influences both parameters through path length and available capacity. A collective built from many sequential rounds of messages pays $\\MessageStartupLatency$ once per round, so its startup cost can grow with the number of participants even when the underlying fabric provides short physical paths.</p>\n<p>↪ The same decomposition, summed over each schedule's rounds, is developed for collective algorithms in @sec-communication-collective-operations-collective-operations-network-performance-modeling-0d8e.</p>\n<p>The model reveals two regimes that call for different engineering responses. When $\\PointToPointMessageSize&lt; \\MessageStartupLatency\\LinkBandwidth$, the startup cost $\\MessageStartupLatency$ exceeds the transmission cost, placing small control messages deep in the latency-bound region, whereas when $\\PointToPointMessageSize&gt; \\MessageStartupLatency\\LinkBandwidth$, the $\\PointToPointMessageSize/\\LinkBandwidth$ term dominates and large gradient transfers sit deep in the bandwidth-bound region. Near the crossover both terms matter, and where a collective lands also depends on how its algorithm divides the payload into messages and schedules them across rounds.</p>\n<p>↪ The crossover message size that separates the latency-dominated and bandwidth-dominated regimes, with a worked InfiniBand NDR example, is developed in @sec-appdx-comm-foundations-regimes.</p>\n<p>For the modeled NDR InfiniBand path with $\\MessageStartupLatency=$ <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.ib_alpha_us_str]</code> and $\\LinkBandwidth=$ <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.ib_beta_gb_per_s_str]</code>, the crossover point $\\LatencyBandwidthCrossoverSize = \\MessageStartupLatency\\cdot \\LinkBandwidth\\approx$ <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.n_cross_ib_kb_str]</code>. Measured startup latency and delivered bandwidth vary with the NIC, switch path, software stack, and contention, so these parameters describe a scenario rather than a protocol constant. Far below the crossover, more bandwidth removes only a small part of transfer time; far above it, lower startup latency removes only a small part. Shorter paths can lower $\\MessageStartupLatency$, while additional delivered path bandwidth raises $\\LinkBandwidth$; their value depends on the message sizes the workload actually exchanges.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: A fabric designer is comparing a modeled InfiniBand NDR path (<code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.ib_alpha_us_str]</code>, <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.ib_beta_gb_per_s_str]</code>) against a modeled RoCE path over slower Ethernet (<code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.eth_alpha_us_str]</code>, <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.eth_beta_gb_per_s_str]</code>). The comparison deliberately bundles a higher-latency stack with a slower link, so a third path, RoCE on a <code>[AlphaBetaSameRate.beta_gbit_per_s_str]</code> link, separates the two effects. For a <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.n_small_kb_str]</code> control message and a <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.n_large_mb_str]</code> gradient shard, which part of $\\PointToPointTransferTime(\\PointToPointMessageSize)=\\MessageStartupLatency+\\PointToPointMessageSize/\\LinkBandwidth$ dominates, and why does the first path help for different reasons in each regime?</p>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>: Apply $\\PointToPointTransferTime(\\PointToPointMessageSize) = \\MessageStartupLatency+ \\PointToPointMessageSize/\\LinkBandwidth$ for a <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.n_small_kb_str]</code> control message and a <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.n_large_mb_str]</code> gradient shard.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Small message</strong> (<code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.n_small_kb_str]</code>):</li>\n</ol>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>InfiniBand: <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.small_ib_eq_math]</code></li>\n<li>RoCE (100G): <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.small_eth_eq_math]</code></li>\n<li><strong>Result</strong>: Under these assumed path parameters, the NDR path is <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.small_speedup_mult_str]</code> faster because its modeled $\\MessageStartupLatency$ is lower.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Large message</strong> (<code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.n_large_mb_str]</code>):</li>\n</ol>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>InfiniBand: $\\PointToPointTransferTime= \\MessageStartupLatency+ \\PointToPointMessageSize/\\LinkBandwidth=$ <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.t_large_ib_ms_str]</code></li>\n<li>RoCE (100G): $\\PointToPointTransferTime= \\MessageStartupLatency+ \\PointToPointMessageSize/\\LinkBandwidth=$ <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.t_large_eth_ms_str]</code></li>\n<li><strong>Result</strong>: Under these assumed path parameters, the NDR path is <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.large_speedup_mult_str]</code> faster because its modeled $\\LinkBandwidth$ is higher. That factor is the ratio of the two line rates, so it measures the faster link rather than the transport.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Same line rate</strong> (RoCE at <code>[AlphaBetaSameRate.beta_gbit_per_s_str]</code>, RoCE $\\MessageStartupLatency$):</li>\n</ol>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Small message: <code>[AlphaBetaSameRate.t_small_us_str]</code>, still <code>[AlphaBetaSameRate.small_ratio_mult_str]</code> the NDR time, because the assumed $\\MessageStartupLatency$ is all that separates these two paths.</li>\n<li>Large message: <code>[AlphaBetaSameRate.t_large_ms_str]</code>, essentially the NDR time, because at equal $\\LinkBandwidth$ nothing but a few microseconds of $\\MessageStartupLatency$ separates them.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: This scenario places the crossover $(\\LatencyBandwidthCrossoverSize = \\MessageStartupLatency\\LinkBandwidth)$ near <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.n_cross_ib_kb_str]</code>. Because gradients are megabytes to gigabytes, their transfers are usually bandwidth dominated, whereas pipeline coordination can remain latency dominated. A higher line rate produces little benefit for a message whose startup term dominates; transport processing, topology, and hop count then govern the improvement. At equal line rate the two stacks in this scenario differ on the control message and barely at all on the gradient shard, so a recommendation between them weighs how much step time small messages consume against the operational burden of each stack (@tbl-network-fabrics-rdma-stacks).</p>\n<p>The two message sizes in notebook \\ref{nbk-network-fabrics-alpha-beta-crossover} map directly onto the 175B training job. For the <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.n_small_kb_str]</code> control message that notifies a pipeline stage its next microbatch is ready ($\\PointToPointTransferTime(\\PointToPointMessageSize) \\approx$ <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.t_small_ib_us_str]</code> on NDR), the transmission term contributes only <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.small_bw_pct_str]</code> of the total, so faster links offer little benefit. For the rounded <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.n_large_grad_mb_str]</code> shard that stands for one share of the <code>[NetworkRunningExampleCluster.grad_gb_str]</code> gradient across the <code>[NetworkRunningExampleCluster.n_gpus_str]</code> ($\\PointToPointTransferTime(\\PointToPointMessageSize) \\approx$ <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.t_large_ib_ms_str]</code> for that one message), transmission dominates, and doubling bandwidth to <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.doubled_beta_gb_per_s_str]</code> approximately halves the transfer time.</p>\n<p>Across the full range of message sizes, each path traces a flat latency-dominated plateau that bends into a linear bandwidth-dominated regime (@fig-alpha-beta-crossover). On the modeled NDR path, the knee at <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.n_cross_ib_kb_str]</code> marks where transmission time overtakes the <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.ib_alpha_us_str]</code> startup latency; the <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.eth_beta_gbit_per_s_str]</code> RoCE path sits on a higher floor of <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.eth_alpha_us_str]</code> and bends near <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.n_cross_eth_kb_str]</code>. The exact positions reflect the assumed $\\MessageStartupLatency$ and $\\LinkBandwidth$ scenario parameters rather than invariant protocol constants. On both paths, startup latency governs sub-kilobyte control handoffs, whereas multi-megabyte gradient shards sit well inside the bandwidth-bound regime.</p>\n<p>Beyond classifying individual transfers, the same model identifies when a collective overtakes computation. A ring AllReduce, one common schedule, passes chunks around a logical cycle of participants, in a ReduceScatter phase that leaves each participant one chunk of the combined result and then an AllGather phase, with no requirement that the physical switches form a ring. The scaling laws that push training toward more parameters (@sec-vol2-intro-ai-scaling-laws-a043) enlarge, in direct proportion, the gradient that every data-parallel step must reduce. Summed over the ring's rounds, the $\\MessageStartupLatency$-$\\LinkBandwidth$ cost of each transfer then decides when that collective outlasts the step's computation. At that point the network becomes the pacing constraint for the entire job.</p>\n<p>↪ The ring AllReduce derivation, and the schedules that compete with it, are developed in @sec-communication-collective-operations-collective-operations-ring-allreduce-ffce.</p>\n<p>A measured collective shows both regimes and where the two-parameter model needs care. NVIDIA publishes the full output of the standard NCCL AllReduce benchmark on one node of eight H100 GPUs connected by NVLink, sweeping message sizes from 8 bytes to 16 GB [@nvidia2026exemplarnccl]. The run crosses no external network, so it tests the two-parameter form itself, free of external switch paths and fabric congestion. The two-parameter model can be built from two summary values of that sweep, the median time of the small messages as $\\MessageStartupLatency$ and the delivered rate of the largest message as $\\LinkBandwidth$, and set beside the measured time (@fig-nccl-allreduce-measured). The small messages sit on a floor of about <code>[MeasuredAllReduce.floor_us_str]</code> µs regardless of size, and the largest messages approach <code>[MeasuredAllReduce.bw_str]</code> of algorithm bandwidth, defined as the tensor size divided by completion time.</p>\n<p>The benchmark multiplies that algorithm bandwidth by $2(8-1)/8 = 1.75$, the bytes a ring AllReduce sends across each GPU's links per tensor byte reduced, to report <code>[MeasuredAllReduce.peak_busbw_str]</code> of bus bandwidth. The benchmark applies this factor whichever schedule ran, so the figure is a normalization rather than a link measurement, and it can exceed NVLink's <code>[NetworkFabricsContextScenario.nvlink_h100_perdir_gb_per_s_str]</code> per direction.</p>\n<p>↪ In-network reduction, in which switches rather than GPUs combine contributions and which NVLink SHARP brings to the node's NVLink switches, is developed in @sec-communication-sharp.</p>\n<p>Between the two extremes, the model places this fitted collective's knee near <code>[MeasuredAllReduce.knee_mb_str]</code> MB, far above the <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.n_cross_ib_kb_str]</code> crossover of a single NDR path, but the measured time runs up to <code>[MeasuredAllReduce.mid_ratio_mult_str]</code> the model's prediction for messages between roughly 1 MB and 1 GB. Across that middle range, chunks are not yet large enough to fill every parallel NVLink channel and amortize per-round protocol overhead, so delivered bandwidth is still ramping toward its peak. The model is therefore close at the two ends and optimistic in the middle, and that middle range is where the gradient buckets (the groups of gradient tensors reduced in one call) of data-parallel training often fall.</p>\n<p>Consider the 175B job on the <code>[NetworkRunningExampleCluster.n_gpus_value_str]</code>-GPU reference cluster. Its gradient exchanges move messages of hundreds of megabytes, while its pipeline stages coordinate through frequent control messages of a few kilobytes.</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Protocol fit</strong>: use the $\\MessageStartupLatency$-$\\LinkBandwidth$ crossover to recommend a transport stack, and justify the choice for both the large gradient messages and the small control messages.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>RoCE requirements</strong>: compare the infrastructure required by PFC-based and PFC-free RoCE designs with InfiniBand's credit-based fabric.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Line rate vs. startup cost</strong>: explain why moving the control messages from a 100 Gb/s path to a 400 Gb/s path with the same $\\MessageStartupLatency$ would barely change their transfer time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The $\\MessageStartupLatency$-$\\LinkBandwidth$ model quantifies the speed of a single link, but the 175B-parameter model requires <code>[NetworkTransportBridgeRecap.n_gpus_str]</code> to work in concert. Scaling these transport primitives from a pair of nodes to a warehouse-scale supercomputer demands a <strong>network topology</strong>: a specific pattern of connections that maximizes $\\BisectionBandwidth$ while minimizing the hop count and cabling cost for global collective operations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h2\">Level 3 Switch and Topology</h2>\n<p>A cluster can have thousands of fast links at the edge and still starve its AllReduce operations if the cross-sectional capacity at the narrowest point in the switching hierarchy is insufficient. The physical arrangement of switches determines whether that cross-section throttles collective communication or sustains full line-rate exchange across all participating workers. Two properties govern this capability: bisection bandwidth $\\BisectionBandwidth$ and whether the fabric is non-blocking. The bisection bandwidth theorem (principle \\ref{pri-bisection-bandwidth}) captures the constraint, stating that traffic crossing a cut is limited by the aggregate bandwidth of that cut. Bisection bandwidth quantifies the worst-case throughput ceiling that the topology imposes on global collectives.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Bisection bandwidth</strong></em> is a network topology metric defined as the minimum aggregate link capacity crossing any partition that divides the cluster into two equal halves, representing the worst-case throughput ceiling for traffic that must cross that partition, including global collectives.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: Bisection bandwidth $\\BisectionBandwidth$ directly sets the cluster-scale bandwidth ceiling for global synchronization. A <code>[BisectionDefinitionExample.gpus_value_str]</code>-GPU fat-tree with <code>[BisectionDefinitionExample.link_gbit_per_s_str]</code> links at 1:1 subscription provides <code>[BisectionDefinitionExample.bisect_1_1_math]</code> per direction; a <code>[BisectionDefinitionExample.oversub_ratio_str]</code>:1 oversubscribed spine reduces this to <code>[BisectionDefinitionExample.bisect_4_1_tb_per_s_str]</code>, increasing the transfer-time lower bound for a fixed cut-crossing payload by <code>[BisectionDefinitionExample.slowdown_mult_str]</code>. Actual AllReduce slowdown depends on rank placement, the collective schedule, and which links are saturated.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Unlike aggregate bandwidth (the sum of all edge link speeds, which can be high even in a poorly connected topology), $\\BisectionBandwidth$ measures global connectivity. A star topology with 1,000 edge links all meeting at one central switch has high aggregate bandwidth but $\\BisectionBandwidth$ limited by that switch's backplane capacity.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: A frequent misconception is that adding more leaf switches always increases $\\BisectionBandwidth$. In a three-tier fat-tree, oversubscribing the spine layer (giving each spine switch fewer uplinks to the core than downlinks to its leaves) reduces $\\BisectionBandwidth$ below the edge-link total regardless of how many leaf switches are present.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>ML training includes global collectives and AllToAll phases that can drive traffic across topology partitions, making full $\\BisectionBandwidth$ a useful capacity target. A smaller cut becomes a bottleneck when the workload's crossing traffic exceeds what it can deliver within the communication budget, and rank placement together with the collective schedule determines how much traffic crosses that cut.</p>\n<p>Bisection bandwidth measures cut capacity; a non-blocking fabric provides paths that can sustain simultaneous input-output pairings at line rate with suitable routing. Full $\\BisectionBandwidth$ and balanced uplink capacity support that goal, but routing must also spread traffic across the available paths. In the oversubscribed designs considered here, $\\BisectionBandwidth$ shrinks by a factor equal to the oversubscription ratio. The transfer-time bound for a fixed payload that saturates the cut rises by that same factor, whereas a collective that keeps most traffic within each half need not experience the same slowdown.</p>\n<p>↪ A slower cut lengthens the training step only when the extra transfer time cannot hide behind computation. The communication-to-compute diagnostic that tests this is developed in @sec-appdx-c3-taxonomy-comp-comm.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Non-blocking fabric</strong></em> is a network with sufficient internal path capacity to support any permutation of input-output port pairs simultaneously at full line rate under a suitable routing assignment.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: In ML fleets, a non-blocking fabric can support full-rate permutation traffic with suitable routing, preserving the available $\\BisectionBandwidth$ term for global collectives. A <code>[NonBlockingOversubscription.oversub_ratio_str]</code>:1 oversubscribed spine, a common data-center compromise, halves the effective $\\BisectionBandwidth$, doubling the communication time when that cut is the saturated bottleneck and dropping scaling efficiency $\\ScalingEfficiency$ accordingly. In a scenario workload assumed to spend <code>[NonBlockingOversubscription.comm_pct_str]</code> of each step communicating, doubling that share while the computation share stays fixed lengthens each step, and the longer step costs roughly <code>[NonBlockingOversubscription.throughput_loss_pct_str]</code> of total cluster throughput. The bisection definition above and notebook \\ref{nbk-network-fabrics-bisection-bandwidth-cost-oversubscription} use <code>[BisectionDefinitionExample.oversub_ratio_str]</code>:1 instead, a cost-optimized spine; the notebook writes out the throughput formula behind both results.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: The guarantee concerns a permutation, in which each input and output belongs to one pairing. It does not promise full line rate from every sender to every receiver at once, nor does it make an arbitrary routing assignment free of contention.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: A frequent misconception is that non-blocking means zero congestion. Endpoint congestion (incast) can still occur if multiple senders simultaneously target the same receiver port, regardless of how much internal fabric capacity is available.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Fat-trees build on Clos-style non-blocking network principles to supply full $\\BisectionBandwidth$ [@clos1953], rail-optimized networks trade some global routing flexibility for lower cabling cost, and dragonfly topologies reduce optical link counts while introducing workload-placement constraints [@kim2008]. This trade-off between guaranteed bisection bandwidth and cabling cost drives every topology decision in large-scale ML clusters, as the comparisons in @sec-network-fabrics-fat-tree, @sec-network-fabrics-rail-optimized, and @sec-network-fabrics-dragonfly show, and every switched design begins at the first-hop switch that aggregates a rack or rail of servers.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-15\" class=\"cs-h3\">Top-of-rack (ToR) and the failure domain</h3>\n<p>Consider a rack containing four nodes with eight GPUs each: if all 32 accelerators attach to a single top-of-rack (ToR)} switch with no alternate path, losing that switch disconnects the entire rack from the job at once. Because the ToR aggregates every server link in the rack, it becomes a shared failure domain, one level of the physical hierarchy defined in @sec-compute-infrastructure-node-health-reliability-56a6, and recovery then depends on available paths, surviving state, and the job's recovery policy. This one-switch-per-rack wiring is the simpler baseline used here to teach radix and failure domains. The reference cluster itself wires each GPU to a rail switch, a layout developed in @sec-network-fabrics-rail-optimized. That layout reshapes the failure domain, because losing one rail switch cuts one GPU in each of many nodes rather than every GPU in one rack.</p>\n<p>The same boundary shapes state placement. To survive a rack-wide outage, a job needs recoverable state outside that rack and a way to resume on available capacity. Topology-aware scheduling must therefore consider both fast communication paths and the failures that several nearby resources share.</p>\n<p>↪ Nested failure domains, and why redundant state must span them to survive a correlated rack or ToR outage, are developed in @sec-fault-tolerance-reliability-reliability-correlated-failures-95e8.</p>\n<p>How many accelerators share one first-hop failure domain depends on the switch <strong>radix</strong>, its number of ports, which determines how many endpoints and uplinks a single switch can connect. In the reference allocation, a <code>[NetworkTopologyRadixRecap.radix_str]</code>-port switch assigns <code>[NetworkTopologyRadixRecap.downlinks_str]</code> ports to servers, one for each of <code>[NetworkTopologyRadixRecap.downlinks_str]</code> GPUs, and <code>[NetworkTopologyRadixRecap.uplinks_str]</code> ports to the spine. With equal port rates, the 1:1 ratio avoids oversubscription at that boundary; delivered throughput still depends on forwarding and the paths beyond it. The <code>[NetworkRunningExampleCluster.n_gpus_value_str]</code>-GPU cluster therefore requires approximately <code>[NetworkTopologyRadixRecap.n_leaf_groups_str]</code> such switches, although the physical rack count also depends on node density and the cabling layout.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Switch radix</strong>: High-end switches such as NVIDIA Quantum-2 offer a radix of <code>[NetworkTopologyRadixRecap.radix_str]</code> ports, each at <code>[IbNdrWireRecap.ib_ndr_gbit_per_s_str]</code>. As model scale pushes toward 100,000 GPUs, higher radix (128 or 256 ports), larger local accelerator domains, or accepted topology constraints are the main ways to avoid adding switch tiers, each of which adds switch latency and cost.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h3\">Fat-tree (Clos) networks</h3>\n<p>The ToR provides non-blocking bandwidth within a single rack, but connecting the reference cluster's <code>[NetworkTopologyRadixRecap.n_leaf_groups_str]</code> first-hop switches into a cluster that preserves full $\\BisectionBandwidth$ across every possible communication pair requires a topology that scales cross-sectional capacity with cluster size. The fat-tree (also called a Clos network) achieves this by adding parallel switches at each tier above the leaves, so the aggregate uplink capacity always matches the total edge bandwidth feeding into it. In this vocabulary, leaf switches attach servers or racks, spine switches connect leaves within a pod, and core switches connect multiple pods when a third tier is needed; other sources call the leaf and spine tiers edge and aggregation. A pod here is a switching domain, the group of leaves that one set of spines joins, which can be narrower than the multi-rack pod of @sec-compute-pod. A multi-rack pod in that sense can contain several fat-tree pods.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Fat-tree</strong></em>} is a hierarchical ML cluster network topology in which the number of parallel paths (and therefore aggregate cross-sectional capacity) increases at each switch tier toward the spine, providing full $\\BisectionBandwidth$ and multiple equal-cost routes between any two nodes [@al-fares2008].</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: A $\\SwitchRadix$-ary fat-tree built from radix-$\\SwitchRadix$ switches supports $\\SwitchRadix^2/2$ hosts in a standalone two-tier configuration and $\\SwitchRadix^3/4$ hosts in a three-tier configuration with full $\\BisectionBandwidth$. With <code>[NetworkTopologyRadixRecap.radix_str]</code>-port switches, a standalone two-tier fabric supports <code>[NetworkTopologyRadixRecap.two_tier_hosts_str]</code> GPUs and a three-tier fabric supports <code>[NetworkTopologyRadixRecap.three_tier_hosts_str]</code> hosts. This full-bisection guarantee is a capacity bound for suitable routing and admissible traffic; it does not eliminate endpoint contention or guarantee that a particular AllReduce implementation reaches line rate.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Unlike a standard tree (where bandwidth at the root is a single bottleneck shared by all leaves), a fat-tree replaces each root with multiple spine switches whose combined uplink capacity matches the total edge bandwidth, eliminating the bottleneck.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: A frequent misconception is that fat-trees guarantee zero network cost. They require $\\mathcal{O}(\\TopologyEndpointCount\\log \\TopologyEndpointCount)$ switches and dense cabling, so a non-blocking three-tier fat-tree at the 4,000-GPU scale needs hundreds of switches and more than ten thousand links before a single accelerator is purchased.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A three-tier radix-4 fabric ($\\SwitchRadix=4$) shows the non-blocking structure at a scale small enough to draw (@fig-fat-tree-detail), with four core switches interconnecting four dashed pod modules. Leaf and spine switches split their ports between the tiers below and above; core switches use all their ports to connect pods. Each tier boundary therefore carries 16 links. The highlighted route traverses five switches and is one of $(\\SwitchRadix/2)^2 = 4$ equal-cost routes from a GPU in Pod 1 to Pod 4. Parallel paths supply the capacity, while routing must distribute traffic across them to use it.</p>\n<p>The fat-tree is a common default for ML clusters because a non-blocking design can provide full $\\BisectionBandwidth$ across arbitrary partitions. This capacity benefits global AllReduce even though implementations such as rings and trees do not require every pair of ranks to communicate at once. The number of hosts that the guarantee can cover depends on switch radix and tier count.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Fat-tree (Clos network)</strong>: The underlying multi-stage switching theory was invented by @clos1953 at Bell Labs to minimize the number of electromechanical crosspoints in telephone exchanges. @leiserson1985 at MIT generalized the concept as the \"fat-tree,\" where the tree is \"fat\" because link bandwidth increases toward the root, proving it could emulate any network of equal hardware volume. The same cost-minimization logic that drove 1950s telephony now drives ML cluster design: minimize switch count while guaranteeing non-blocking connectivity for global AllReduce.</p>\n<p>A fat-tree built from switches with radix $\\SwitchRadix$ supports $\\FatTreeHostCount= \\SwitchRadix^{3}/4$ hosts at three tiers. A standalone two-tier non-blocking fabric can instead use $\\SwitchRadix$ leaves, each with $\\SwitchRadix/2$ host ports and $\\SwitchRadix/2$ uplinks, connected to $\\SwitchRadix/2$ spines. Each spine uses all $\\SwitchRadix$ ports to reach the leaves, providing $\\SwitchRadix^{2}/2 =$ <code>[FatTreeScaling.fat_tree_two_tier_hosts_str]</code> host ports for radix-64 switches. In the three-tier design, each spine switch reserves half of its ports for the core tier and uses the other $\\SwitchRadix/2$ ports to connect to leaves, so one pod has $\\SwitchRadix/2$ leaves and $(\\SwitchRadix/2)^2 =$ <code>[FatTreeScaling.fat_tree_pod_hosts_str]</code> host ports. Because each radix-$\\SwitchRadix$ core switch connects one port to each pod, the core tier can join up to $\\SwitchRadix$ pods for the full $\\SwitchRadix \\times (\\SwitchRadix/2)^2 = \\SwitchRadix^3/4$ host capacity. These are different port allocations and switch counts rather than two ways of counting the same fabric, and while the <code>[NetworkFatTreeRecap.n_gpus_str]</code>-GPU reference cluster fits within a standalone two-tier design, the distinction matters as soon as a cluster outgrows that limit.</p>\n<p>A <code>[FatTreeScaling.scale_out_hosts_str]</code>-GPU deployment cannot join two fully populated standalone <code>[FatTreeScaling.fat_tree_two_tier_hosts_str]</code>-host fabrics at their spines, because every spine port is already occupied by a leaf connection. Instead, it is built as <code>[FatTreeScaling.scale_out_pods_word_str]</code> <code>[FatTreeScaling.fat_tree_pod_hosts_str]</code>-host pods of the three-tier design, joined through a core tier. A concrete bisection estimate (notebook \\ref{nbk-network-fabrics-bisection-bandwidth-cost-oversubscription}) shows how quickly oversubscription at that spine or core boundary turns into synchronization delay.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: A cluster designer is choosing between a \"non-blocking\" (1:1) fat-tree and a \"cost-optimized\" (<code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_oversubscription_ratio_str]</code>:1) spine for a <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_cluster_cost_m_str]</code> cluster of <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_n_gpus_str]</code>. This is a separate economics scenario rather than the running 175B job: each GPU contributes a <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_gradient_gb_str]</code> gradient payload, and the workload is assumed to spend $\\CommunicationTimeFraction=$ <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_comm_fraction_pct_str]</code> of each step communicating. That share is a scenario input. It is larger than the 13 percent of the illustrative schedule in @fig-bandwidth-cliff-gantt, and neither is a measured share of the reference job. How much slower will the AllReduce be on the cheaper network, and what does that cost?</p>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>: Bisection bandwidth ($\\BisectionBandwidth$) is the minimum pipe diameter between halves of the cluster. Assume a unidirectional ring whose ranks alternate between the two halves of the cluster. Every ring edge then crosses the cut, with $\\AcceleratorCount/2$ senders in each direction. Assume balanced routing, no competing traffic, and negligible startup latency. ReduceScatter plus AllGather contributes a traffic factor $2(\\AcceleratorCount-1)/\\AcceleratorCount$ = <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_ring_factor_str]</code> per GPU; multiplying by $\\AcceleratorCount/2$ gives the bytes crossing the cut in each direction. With a <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_gradient_gb_str]</code> payload, a traffic factor of <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_ring_factor_str]</code>, and <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_gpus_per_side_value_str]</code> senders per direction, <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_total_data_gb_str]</code> crosses the cut. A ring that groups ranks within each half would have fewer crossing edges and a different cut-load bound.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Non-blocking (1:1)</strong>: $\\BisectionBandwidth =$ <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_gpus_per_side_value_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_beta_gb_per_s_str]</code> $=$ <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_bw_1_1_gb_per_s_str]</code> per direction.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Time: <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_total_data_gb_str]</code> / <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_bw_1_1_gb_per_s_str]</code> $\\approx$ <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_time_1_1_s_str]</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Oversubscribed (<code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_oversubscription_ratio_str]</code>:1)</strong>: $\\BisectionBandwidth =$ <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_bw_1_1_gb_per_s_str]</code> divided by <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_oversubscription_ratio_str]</code> = <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_bw_4_1_gb_per_s_str]</code> per direction.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Time: <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_total_data_gb_str]</code> / <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_bw_4_1_gb_per_s_str]</code> $\\approx$ <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_time_4_1_s_str]</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Throughput</strong>: If the communication share stretches by the slowdown factor $\\CommunicationSlowdownFactor$ while computation stays fixed, then <code>[BisectionThroughputStep.rel_eq_math]</code>, a loss of about <code>[BisectionThroughputStep.loss_pct_str]</code> of cluster throughput.</li>\n<li><strong>Cost</strong>: That loss times the <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_cluster_cost_m_str]</code> capital cost is about <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_waste_m_str]</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: For this cut-saturating placement, saving money on spine switches creates a <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_slowdown_mult_str]</code> bottleneck for global synchronization, and on a <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_cluster_cost_m_str]</code> cluster that bottleneck idles about <code>[BisectionScaling.bbs_waste_m_str]</code> of compute. The same formula gives the <code>[NonBlockingOversubscription.throughput_loss_pct_str]</code> loss of the <code>[NonBlockingOversubscription.oversub_ratio_str]</code>:1 spine in the non-blocking definition. This is an equivalent-capacity cost under the stated workload assumptions, not a direct equipment loss. Oversubscription must be evaluated against the collective traffic actually crossing the constrained cut.</p>\n<p>⇄ <strong>Contrast:</strong> The GPT-3 synchronization example in @sec-introduction-energyscale-invariant-a0d6 assumes pure data parallelism and no overlap, so its exposed communication share far exceeds the share assumed here.</p>\n<p>The subscription ratio at each tier therefore determines whether a fat-tree can sustain global collectives, and the tier count determines how far it can grow. Scaling beyond the <code>[FatTreeScaling.fat_tree_two_tier_hosts_str]</code>-host two-tier limit requires a three-tier architecture with core switches, enabling the fabric to reach <code>[FatTreeScaling.fat_tree_hosts_str]</code> hosts, but the added scale carries both cost and latency. Each of the $\\SwitchRadix$ pods contains $\\SwitchRadix/2$ leaf switches and $\\SwitchRadix/2$ spine switches ($\\SwitchRadix^2$ switches across all pods), and their $\\SwitchRadix/2$ spines each send $\\SwitchRadix/2$ uplinks into $(\\SwitchRadix/2)^2 = \\SwitchRadix^2/4$ core switches. A non-blocking $\\SwitchRadix=64$ three-tier tree therefore requires $5\\SwitchRadix^2/4 =$ <code>[FatTreeScaling.fat_tree_switches_str]</code> switches across the core, spine, and leaf layers. At <code>[FatTreeScaling.switch_cost_low_str]</code> to <code>[FatTreeScaling.switch_cost_high_str]</code> per switch, the switching layer alone represents <code>[FatTreeScaling.infra_cost_low_m_str]</code> to <code>[FatTreeScaling.infra_cost_high_m_str]</code>. Typical hop counts also rise, from two inter-switch links and three switch traversals for intra-pod traffic (leaf-spine-leaf) to four inter-switch links and five switch traversals for inter-pod traffic (leaf-spine-core-spine-leaf), adding serialization delay and switch processing time to the $\\MessageStartupLatency$ latency term across thousands of synchronization steps. Rail-optimized topologies respond to both pressures by matching the physical cabling pattern to the collective communication pattern.</p>\n<p>These questions check whether hierarchical switch-fabric trade-offs are clear:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Radix and scale</strong>: compute the maximum number of GPUs a radix-64 two-tier fat-tree can connect without core switches.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Non-blocking ratio</strong>: explain why a non-blocking fabric requires a 1:1 subscription ratio at every tier of the network.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Tier vs. latency</strong>: determine how $\\MessageStartupLatency$ (startup latency) changes when moving from a two-tier to a three-tier fat-tree.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Aggregate vs. bisection</strong>: distinguish aggregate bandwidth from $\\BisectionBandwidth$ and identify the metric that matters for AllReduce.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h3\">Rail-optimized topology</h3>\n<p>When tensor-parallel groups stay inside each node's high-bandwidth NVSwitch domain, data parallelism exchanges gradients only between GPUs that hold the same model shard in the same local slot position across nodes, yet a standard fat-tree wires all eight GPUs of a node into a shared ToR switch. A <strong>rail-optimized topology</strong> instead cables external network ports by local slot position (@fig-rail-optimized-cabling): GPU 0 in every node connects to the Rail 0 switch, GPU 1 connects to Rail 1, and so on across the cluster.</p>\n<p>This slot-aligned wiring exploits the stratified traffic that 3D parallelism produces. Because each node holds one full tensor-parallel group, with one shard on each GPU, each data-parallel replica assigns the corresponding model shard to the same local GPU slot (where the slot number is a local rank within a node, distinct from the worker's global rank in the distributed job). Replicas are identical copies of one layout, so nodes that hold the same pipeline stage hold the same slice of the model in a given slot. During data-parallel AllReduce, GPU 0 on one node synchronizes its shard's gradients with GPU 0 on other nodes, but rarely communicates with GPU 1. Isolating each local rank onto its own rail switch fabric removes same-rank traffic from shared rack switches and, when a rail fits on a single switch, shortens the path between corresponding GPUs. The isolation holds however a rail is built, whereas the shorter path does not.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: A team is synchronizing per-rank data-parallel gradients across <code>[RailLatency.rol_n_nodes_str]</code>. In a standard fat-tree, each message between same-rank GPUs traverses its source leaf switch, a spine switch, and the destination leaf switch (<code>[RailLatency.rol_std_hops_str]</code> switch traversals). In a rail-optimized network built from rail switches with at least one port per node, all corresponding GPUs sit on the same rail switch (one traversal). On the reference cluster that requires rail switches with more ports than the radix-<code>[NetworkTopologyRadixRecap.radix_str]</code> switch used so far, so the estimate describes a higher-radix design. How much latency dividend does the rail design earn when a rail fits on one switch?</p>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>: This estimate counts only the switch-traversal contribution to communication latency ($\\MessageStartupLatency$), using the same assumed delay for each traversal.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Standard latency</strong>: <code>[RailLatency.rol_std_hops_str]</code> traversals $\\times$ <code>[RailLatency.rol_hop_lat_us_str]</code> = <code>[RailLatency.rol_std_lat_us_str]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Rail-optimized</strong>: <code>[RailLatency.rol_rail_hops_str]</code> traversal $\\times$ <code>[RailLatency.rol_hop_lat_us_str]</code> = <code>[RailLatency.rol_rail_lat_us_str]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Result</strong>: <code>[RailLatency.rol_speedup_mult_str]</code> lower latency.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: For synchronous data-parallel AllReduce, this <code>[RailLatency.rol_speedup_mult_str]</code> reduction applies to the modeled switch-latency contribution, not to total collective time. Its effect on scaling efficiency ($\\ScalingEfficiency$) depends on payload size, delivered bandwidth, compute time, and overlap. The single-switch rail in this example avoids the spine traversal for same-rank traffic. The dividend is conditional. On the reference cluster's radix-<code>[NetworkTopologyRadixRecap.radix_str]</code> switches each rail outgrows one switch and regains the extra traversals, so what rails reliably buy there is isolation, developed below.</p>\n<p>The hop saving is only part of the case for rails. Large language model fleets adopt the pattern because it matches how their parallelism plan divides traffic.</p>\n<p>Archetype A's use of 3D parallelism generates two distinct traffic patterns that pull in opposite directions: (1) large, bandwidth-hungry gradient averaging for data parallelism, and (2) high-frequency, latency-sensitive activation exchanges for tensor parallelism. Keeping tensor parallelism inside each node's NVLink domain leaves data-parallel gradient traffic on the fabric, and rails give each same-rank data-parallel group its own switch network. A rail can provide a single switch hop between nodes when its endpoint count fits the available switch ports. Larger rails require additional switching stages, so rail alignment alone does not guarantee single-hop communication. Either way, the lesson for Archetype A is that how the fabric is wired to the parallelism plan matters as much as how many accelerators it connects.</p>\n<p>For the cluster of <code>[NetworkRailRecap.n_gpus_str]</code> spanning <code>[NetworkRailRecap.n_nodes_str]</code>, rail alignment creates eight parallel networks of <code>[NetworkRailRecap.gpus_per_rail_str]</code> each, requiring at least one switch port per endpoint in each rail. A single-switch realization therefore needs a radix at least as large as the rail endpoint count; the earlier radix-<code>[NetworkTopologyRadixRecap.radix_str]</code> switch cannot realize this rail alone, which is why notebook \\ref{nbk-network-fabrics-rail-optimized-dividend} assumed higher-radix rail switches. Built from radix-<code>[NetworkTopologyRadixRecap.radix_str]</code> switches instead, each rail becomes a small two-tier fabric that preserves rank alignment but adds hops. For the bandwidth-hungry gradient exchanges of data parallelism, the larger benefit is isolation: each rail carries only its own rank's traffic, so the eight rail networks operate concurrently without competing for shared spine capacity. When a rail fits on one switch, the shorter path also trims the startup cost a ring pays on every round.</p>\n<p>That same isolation creates a sharp trade-off, because traffic that must reach GPUs of different ranks requires bridging across rails. Such traffic includes expert routing in a mixture of experts (MoE) model, which sends each token to the few expert subnetworks chosen for it, and pipeline-stage handoffs that do not align with the rail wiring. Large clusters therefore often employ a hybrid approach, using rail-optimized leaves for same-rank data-parallel traffic while bridging the rails with a full fat-tree spine to support cross-rank communication patterns.</p>\n<p>↪ Expert parallelism, in which each rank hosts a different subset of experts and tokens travel to them by AllToAll, is developed in @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-expert-parallelism-mixture-experts-bc45.</p>\n<p>These questions check whether workload-specific network-design trade-offs are clear:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Rail beneficiary</strong>: identify the dimension of 3D parallelism (tensor parallelism, pipeline parallelism, or data parallelism) that benefits most from a rail-optimized design.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Switch hops</strong>: explain when a rail-optimized network reduces the number of switch hops for gradient exchanges compared to a standard fat-tree, and what it still provides when a rail needs more ports than one switch offers.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Traffic isolation</strong>: explain the traffic isolation benefit of placing all GPU 0s on one rail and all GPU 1s on another.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Core switch need</strong>: classify the claim as true or false: a rail-optimized network eliminates the need for high-bandwidth core switches connecting different rails.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h3\">Dragonfly and torus alternatives</h3>\n<p>Because even a rail-optimized design relies on a fat-tree spine to bridge cross-rank traffic, scaling to tens of thousands of endpoints still forces a third layer of core switches and tens of thousands of long optical cables into the budget, driving network capital expenditure into the tens or hundreds of millions of dollars. Two alternative topologies attack that cost wall from opposite directions: tapering the global switch tier or removing external fabric switches altogether.</p>\n<p>A <strong>dragonfly</strong> topology organizes high-radix routers (the dragonfly literature's name for switches) into fully connected groups joined by global optical links. Each group has $\\DragonflyGroupRouterCount$ routers, and each router has $\\DragonflyTerminalPortCount$ terminal ports and $\\DragonflyGlobalChannelCount$ global channels. The ratio of $\\DragonflyGlobalChannelCount$ to $\\DragonflyTerminalPortCount$ sets how much global bandwidth each group receives relative to the traffic its terminals can inject, so global bandwidth is a design parameter rather than a fixed percentage of aggregate injection bandwidth. The balanced configuration analyzed by @kim2008, $\\DragonflyGroupRouterCount=2\\DragonflyTerminalPortCount=2\\DragonflyGlobalChannelCount$, is one setting of that knob. A tapered dragonfly gives each router fewer global channels than terminal ports, trading cut capacity for fewer long optical links. The routers in a group are fully connected, and the groups are joined by global links. A minimal route between two groups therefore takes at most one local hop to the router holding the right global link, one global hop, and one local hop in the destination group. No minimal path exceeds three router-to-router hops, regardless of system size. Short paths come cheaply, but performance for jobs spanning groups depends on the chosen $\\DragonflyTerminalPortCount/\\DragonflyGlobalChannelCount$ ratio, routing, and traffic matrix, because every inter-group transfer shares the provisioned global links.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Dragonfly topology</strong>: Introduced by @kim2008 at ISCA 2008, the dragonfly uses high-radix routers grouped into fully connected \"super-nodes.\" For systems with at least 16,000 terminals, the architecture achieves a 52 percent network-cost reduction relative to a folded Clos (the fat-tree of @sec-network-fabrics-fat-tree) of equivalent bandwidth, though global optical link oversubscription can create AllToAll bottlenecks during MoE token routing.</p>\n<p>Rather than tapering a router hierarchy, a <strong>torus</strong> topology removes external fabric switches by connecting each node directly to its neighbors in a multidimensional grid, most commonly a 3D torus where every node links to its six adjacent peers (up/down, left/right, front/back). The design offers full local bandwidth with minimal switching hardware, because every neighbor is a single hop away. However, global communication requires traversing the diameter of the mesh ($\\mathcal{O}(\\TopologyEndpointCount^{1/3})$ hops), making latency scale poorly with cluster size. Google adopted this architecture for its Tensor Processing Unit (TPU) pods because the collectives that dominate its training workloads can be scheduled as rings along each torus dimension, a nearest-neighbor pattern that maps naturally onto the physical grid. The limitation becomes apparent with MoE, which relies on AllToAll communication patterns. On a torus, this traffic can congest the limited $\\BisectionBandwidth$ of the mesh and degrade performance relative to a non-blocking switch fabric.</p>\n<p>The trade-off becomes concrete for the <code>[TorusVsFatTree.n_nodes_str]</code>-endpoint deployment that the three-tier fat-tree reached with <code>[FatTreeScaling.scale_out_pods_word_str]</code> pods. Arranged as a <code>[TorusVsFatTree.dims_str]</code> torus with wraparound links, the same endpoints need no external switching stages. Splitting the cube in half across one axis cuts a $16 \\times 16$ plane of interior links and an equal plane of wraparound links, so only $2 \\times 16^2 =$ <code>[TorusVsFatTree.torus_cut_links_str]</code> links cross the bisection, against <code>[TorusVsFatTree.fat_tree_cut_links_str]</code> in the non-blocking fat-tree. With <code>[TorusVsFatTree.link_gb_per_s_str]</code> per link and direction, the torus offers <code>[TorusVsFatTree.torus_bisect_tb_per_s_str]</code> across the cut against the fat-tree's <code>[TorusVsFatTree.fat_tree_bisect_tb_per_s_str]</code>, a <code>[TorusVsFatTree.bisect_ratio_mult_str]</code> gap. Because wraparound links cap the distance along each 16-node axis at 8 hops, the torus's worst-case path across all three axes crosses $3 \\times 8 =$ <code>[TorusVsFatTree.torus_diameter_str]</code> neighbor hops against the fat-tree's <code>[TorusVsFatTree.fat_tree_switches_str]</code> switch traversals. Because a cubic torus's cut grows only as $\\TopologyEndpointCount^{2/3}$ while its endpoint count grows as $\\TopologyEndpointCount$, that capacity gap widens at fixed link rate.</p>\n<p>Whether the gap matters depends on the traffic. A ring AllReduce scheduled along each torus dimension moves data only between neighbors and combines contributions before forwarding them, so it leans little on the narrow cut. An MoE AllToAll or an embedding exchange among a recommendation model's table shards instead sends a large share of its traffic between distant endpoints and drives it straight into the cut, with the exact pressure set by placement and message sizes. Consequently, the collective schedule and traffic matrix determine whether the torus's lower cross-sectional capacity limits completion time.</p>\n<p>↪ AllToAll schedules and their cost at scale, where every rank sends a distinct share to every other rank, are developed in @sec-communication-alltoall.</p>\n<p>At the scale of the <code>[TopologyScenario.n_gpus_str]</code>-GPU reference cluster, the four families range in cut capacity from <code>[TopologyScenario.fat_tree_tb_str]</code> in the non-blocking fat-tree down to <code>[TopologyScenario.torus_tb_str]</code> in a 3D torus, while an assumed interconnect cost per Gb/s of bisection bandwidth runs from <code>[TopologyScenario.torus_cost_str]</code>/Gb/s for the switchless torus to <code>[TopologyScenario.dragonfly_cost_str]</code>/Gb/s for the tapered dragonfly (@fig-network-topologies). The cheapest capacity is also the least capacity, which is the trade every topology decision prices. The dragonfly's assumed cost per Gb/s is the highest here because its savings come from replacing long optical runs and switch tiers at large scale, which @kim2008 reports for systems of at least 16,000 terminals; at <code>[TopologyScenario.n_gpus_str]</code> GPUs it has few such runs to replace. The rail's <code>[TopologyScenario.rail_tb_str]</code> applies to cross-rail traffic only, because same-rank traffic stays on its own rail. Radix, endpoint count, link speed, routing mechanisms, and equipment pricing all shift these trade-offs; the topology family alone dictates neither raw cross-sectional capacity nor total cost.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-19\" class=\"cs-h3\">Optical circuit switching and reconfigurable topology</h3>\n<p>Every topology compared in @fig-network-topologies is fixed when the facility is cabled, locking the cluster into a single physical graph regardless of which workload runs on it. A statically cabled Clos hierarchy (@sec-network-fabrics-fat-tree) fixes the oversubscription ratio and bisection pattern whether the active job is pipeline-dominated, data-parallel, or AllToAll expert-routed. A statically cabled torus (@sec-network-fabrics-dragonfly) is equally rigid: it fits dimension-ordered rings, but it congests under MoE AllToAll traffic and cannot rewire its links to match the job.</p>\n<p>Switching those multi-tier fabrics electrically also adds a recurring power cost at every hop. Each electrical packet switch inspects headers and chooses an outgoing link only after an Optical-Electrical-Optical (OEO) conversion: incoming photons hit a photodiode, high-speed SerDes circuits deserialize the electrical signal, switch ASICs buffer and route packets across crossbars, and output SerDes drive lasers to re-transmit light over optical fiber. At 800 Gb/s and 1.6 Tb/s per-port data rates, SerDes and transceiver electronics consume significant energy (@sec-network-fabrics-serdes), and every additional switch tier repeats that conversion cost for each bit that crosses it. Changing the physical connections between node groups in light, for the duration of a workload allocation rather than packet by packet, avoids both static wiring and per-hop OEO conversion.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Optical circuit switching (OCS)</strong></em> is a physical networking architecture that routes optical signals directly using Micro-Electro-Mechanical Systems (MEMS) mirror arrays without Optical-Electrical-Optical (OEO) conversion, enabling dynamic, software-defined topology reconfiguration and high energy efficiency at warehouse scale.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: An optical circuit bypasses packet-by-packet electronic processing in the switching path. The complete system still consumes power for actuators, control, and transceivers. Its compatibility across line rates depends on optical loss, wavelengths, and endpoint budgets, so protocol transparency does not imply unrestricted upgrades.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Unlike electrical packet switches (which inspect headers, buffer packets in SRAM, and make nanosecond routing decisions per packet), an OCS establishes semi-static physical optical circuits with millisecond-scale switching latency ($10$--$30$ ms), making it suitable for topology-level reconfiguration rather than dynamic packet routing.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: Assuming OCS replaces packet switching entirely. OCS operates at circuit granularity: it configures cluster-scale topologies (such as 3D tori or job-specific sub-clusters) and routes around hardware faults, while packet switching or accelerator-native interconnects handle fine-grained, intra-step message routing.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Google's TPU v4 supercomputer [@jouppi2023] deployed optical circuit switching in production. Instead of wiring 4,096 chips into an inflexible electrical fat-tree, the system groups accelerators into $4{\\times}4{\\times}4$ node cubes and routes the inter-cube links through an array of 3D MEMS optical circuit switches (codenamed Palomar).</p>\n<p>The optical layer serves two purposes, adapting the topology to the workload and recovering from faults. For workload adaptation, the physical interconnect can be reconfigured to match the communication structure of the running model. A training run using 1D pipeline parallelism, whose stages form a line, can stretch its slice along one long axis to streamline stage-to-stage transfers. Conversely, a 2D or 3D tensor-parallel partition, which splits each tensor across a grid of chips, can configure a compact cubic 3D torus that minimizes average hop count, while an MoE workload can maximize bisection bandwidth across specific expert groups.</p>\n<p>Optical switching also supports fault isolation by changing connections around unavailable hardware where spare connectivity permits. The capability matters because in a statically cabled torus, a single failed link or node can break a ring along an entire dimension and degrade every job that spans it. With optical circuit switching, the mirrors can be redirected to bypass the failed cube or fiber and splice healthy cubes into a complete, smaller torus slice without manual recabling. The fabric manager must first detect the fault, choose a feasible allocation, and establish the new circuits, which means circuit-switching time is only one part of recovery; a training job may also need to rebuild its process groups and restore state before computation resumes, so optical reconfiguration latency alone does not specify recovery time.</p>\n<p>The trade-off is switching latency. Because MEMS mirrors physically rotate to steer collimated light beams, reconfiguration occurs on a mechanical timescale that is much longer than packet forwarding in an established electrical path. Circuit setup changes connectivity, while packet forwarding uses connectivity already in place. Consequently, OCS raises the power and protocol ceiling of the switching layer while leaving reconfiguration latency as the limiting constraint, so it is deployed for job-level topology allocation and fault isolation, not for per-message dynamic routing. Within that limit, OCS turns the choice of topology from a decision fixed when the fabric is cabled into one made for each job.</p>\n<p>Each topology comparison so far has considered one traffic pattern at a time. In practice, clusters serve multiple workloads with different communication patterns, and topology selection must balance their competing demands.</p>\n<p>The choice of network topology dictates the upper bound of training efficiency. Warm up by matching each single-workload pattern to its ideal topology, then design a fabric that must serve several at once.</p>\n<p><strong>Single-workload picks</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>AllReduce bandwidth</strong>: select the topology that maximizes $\\BisectionBandwidth$ for a standard data-parallel job dominated by AllReduce.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>MoE traffic</strong>: select the topology that best sustains a mixture-of-experts model's AllToAll-dominant, bursty communication, in which any rank may send to any other.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Neighbor locality</strong>: select the topology that best fits a ring AllReduce scheduled along each dimension of the machine, where every transfer goes to a physical neighbor.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Designing for a mixed cluster</strong></p>\n<p>You are designing the network for a new ML cluster that will run two primary workloads: (1) training a 175B-parameter language model using 3D parallelism (tensor, pipeline, and data parallelism), and (2) serving a mixture-of-experts model that relies heavily on AllToAll communication to route tokens to the correct experts.</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Training topology</strong>: select the topology (fat-tree, rail-optimized, or hybrid) that best matches tensor parallelism within groups of 8 GPUs and data parallelism across all nodes, then justify the choice.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>MoE topology</strong>: revise the topology choice for unpredictable AllToAll traffic where any GPU may need to communicate with any other GPU.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Oversubscription risk</strong>: identify which workload suffers more under 2:1 spine-layer oversubscription and propose a scheduling strategy to mitigate the impact.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Topology provides the structural capacity for the cluster of <code>[NetworkFabricBridgeRecap.n_gpus_str]</code> to communicate, but structure alone does not guarantee performance. When the <code>[NetworkFabricBridgeRecap.n_gpus_str]</code> synchronize contributions to the model's <code>[NetworkRunningExampleCluster.grad_gb_str]</code> gradient state, that theoretical capacity collides with the reality of <strong>fabric behavior</strong>: congestion control and routing dynamics determine whether this traffic flows smoothly or gridlocks under the strict synchronization of the BSP model.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-20\" class=\"cs-h2\">Level 4 Fabric Behavior, Congestion, and Routing</h2>\n<p>A collective can finish late even when the topology provides full $\\BisectionBandwidth$. Levels 1 through 3 determine how long a transfer takes on an unloaded path; fabric behavior determines how much longer it takes once the job's own synchronized traffic loads that path. Competing flows can concentrate on one output path, and the BSP barrier introduced in @sec-network-workload-inversion exposes any delay that extends the last required transfer. The relevant paths are those used by the participating workers; a congested link elsewhere in the fleet need not affect the job. In the Fleet Law (@eq-fleet-law), measured transfer durations contribute to $\\CommunicationTime(\\DeviceCount)$, while residual waiting at synchronization contributes to $\\SynchronizationTime(\\DeviceCount)$, the point where the communication axis of C³ hands its cost to the coordination axis. A timeline must assign each interval consistently so the same congestion delay is not counted twice.</p>\n<p>The p99 tail latency introduced with the BSP barrier helps expose delays that an average hides, but a particular barrier waits for its last required participant, not for a percentile. Evaluating a fabric therefore requires both transfer-time distributions and the collective completion times they produce under load.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-21\" class=\"cs-h3\">Priority flow control (PFC)</h3>\n<p>The BSP weakest-link property means that even a single dropped packet can trigger a retransmission timeout that stalls the global barrier for milliseconds, a delay that can rival the entire communication phase of a training step. PFC-based RoCEv2 fabrics prevent the drop in the first place by running PFC, the pause mechanism introduced with the RDMA stacks (@sec-network-fabrics-ib-roce), in lossless mode, so that upstream senders stop before any buffer saturates [@guo2016rdma; @gangidi2024rdma].</p>\n<p><em><strong>Priority flow control (PFC)</strong></em> is a link-layer mechanism used by RoCEv2 ML cluster fabrics to prevent switch buffer overflow. When a port's queue depth crosses a configured threshold, the switch sends PAUSE frames to an upstream sender, which throttles injection on a per-priority basis without dropping packets.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: In PFC-based RoCEv2 deployments, PFC is the foundation for a lossless Ethernet service. A PFC PAUSE frame must reach the upstream sender before the buffer overflows, so each pause threshold must leave headroom for the data still in flight during roughly one link round trip.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Unlike standard IEEE 802.3 flow control, which pauses all traffic classes on a link, PFC operates per priority class, allowing latency-sensitive control messages to continue flowing while only pausing the congested gradient data queue.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: A frequent misconception is that PFC solves congestion. It transforms packet loss into congestion spreading. PFC itself places no bound on how far PAUSE frames propagate, so a single faulty transceiver can pause traffic far from its own link unless a watchdog intervenes.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The danger of PFC lies in its cascading nature. When a switch port's buffer fills, it sends a PAUSE frame upstream, which can move congestion into the upstream switch and trigger another PAUSE frame. A slow receiver, routing hot spot, or true fan-in burst (many senders converging on one port) can therefore pause links unrelated to the original queue. This behavior, known as <strong>congestion spreading</strong>, is the primary operational risk of PFC-based lossless Ethernet, and the flows it stalls without ever touching the congested queue are called <strong>victim flows</strong>. Fan-in is one trigger, but it is not intrinsic to every collective. A bandwidth-optimal ring exchanges data point to point, although multiple ring flows can still collide at a shared egress or an oversubscribed cut and create a hot spot without a single aggregation receiver (@sec-network-fabrics-incast).</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-22\" class=\"cs-h3\">Lossless fabric hazards and watchdog recovery</h3>\n<p>When that hop-by-hop pause propagation spreads across multiple switch tiers, local congestion spreading can escalate from transient victim-flow stalls into fabric-wide lockup. Because lossless queues cannot drop packets to relieve pressure, backpressure that closes a routing cycle or pins a switch port produces two structural failure modes that do not exist in loss-tolerant packet-switched networks: buffer-dependency deadlocks and PFC storms.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Cyclic buffer dependency deadlock</strong></em> is a permanent distributed lockup in a lossless network, whether its backpressure uses PFC PAUSE frames or InfiniBand credits. A set of switch port queues forms a closed dependency cycle, and each queue holds packets destined for the next queue while backpressure from that same queue pauses it.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: A single such deadlock permanently halts traffic across all participating ports and upstream links, requiring manual intervention or watchdog timeouts that discard packets to recover. InfiniBand fabrics prevent it through deadlock-free routing and virtual-lane assignment, and PFC-based Ethernet fabrics need the same discipline in their routing and priority configuration.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Unlike a PFC storm (which is a transient cascade of backpressure triggered by a slow-draining endpoint), a buffer-dependency deadlock is a static, self-sustaining topological cycle that cannot resolve itself even after traffic injection stops.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: Assuming multi-stage Clos (fat-tree) fabrics are inherently immune to deadlocks. Dynamic multipath routing, link failures, and address resolution flooding can introduce cyclic forwarding dependencies across switch tiers.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4 id=\"sec-23\" class=\"cs-h4\">The graph mechanics of buffer-dependency deadlocks</h4>\n<p>In a lossless network, a switch cannot forward a packet to downstream buffer $\\PacketBuffer_j$ if $\\PacketBuffer_j$ is full; instead, it holds the packet in upstream buffer $\\PacketBuffer_i$ and awaits credit or pause clearance. A directed buffer dependency graph $\\BufferDependencyGraph= (\\BufferDependencyVertexSet, \\BufferDependencyEdgeSet)$ formalizes this relationship. Each vertex $\\PacketBuffer_k \\in \\BufferDependencyVertexSet$ represents a packet buffer or virtual lane queue at a switch port. A directed edge $(\\PacketBuffer_i, \\PacketBuffer_j) \\in \\BufferDependencyEdgeSet$ indicates that a packet currently occupying buffer $\\PacketBuffer_i$ requires buffer $\\PacketBuffer_j$ along its routing path to make forward progress.</p>\n<p>A directed cycle in the buffer dependency graph is a necessary condition for a cyclic buffer dependency deadlock (definition \\ref{dfn-network-fabrics-pfc-deadlock}), but a cycle alone does not establish that progress has stopped. The cyclic dependency in @eq-network-buffer-dependency-cycle becomes a deadlock when the occupied buffers cannot drain and every participant waits for its successor:</p>\n<p>$$\\PacketBuffer_1 \\to \\PacketBuffer_2 \\to \\dots \\to \\PacketBuffer_k \\to \\PacketBuffer_1$$ {#eq-network-buffer-dependency-cycle}</p>\n<p>When each buffer in the cycle fills beyond its configured pause threshold, each port asserts a PFC PAUSE frame to its predecessor. Because no buffer can advance a packet into a paused successor, no buffer can drain, and no port can de-assert its PAUSE signal. The entire cycle freezes permanently. A four-switch ring whose queues ($\\PacketBuffer_1$ through $\\PacketBuffer_4$) are completely full shows this mutual protocol interlock (@fig-pfc-credit-deadlock). Solid gray arrows trace packet forwarding dependencies advancing clockwise ($\\PacketBuffer_1 \\to \\PacketBuffer_2 \\to \\PacketBuffer_3 \\to \\PacketBuffer_4 \\to \\PacketBuffer_1$), while dashed red PAUSE frames propagate counterclockwise. Each full queue pauses its upstream predecessor while waiting for its downstream successor to drain, permanently freezing all four queues until an external watchdog intervenes to drop frames.</p>\n<p>Routing changes and flooding can create the dependencies needed for deadlock. During link failure or route convergence, inconsistent forwarding tables can create transient loops. A lossy IP network eventually discards a looping packet when its time-to-live expires; a lossless class has no such release, so looping traffic can fill the buffers along the loop and hold them. If traffic occupies a cycle of mutually blocked queues, backpressure can prevent progress until recovery breaks the dependency. Multicast or broadcast replication can also fill queues whose dependencies intersect, while cross-connects and asymmetric failover paths can introduce cycles absent from the intended hierarchical route. None of these conditions alone proves a deadlock; the occupied buffers and pause states must form the closed waiting cycle described by the model. Microsoft's production RoCE deployment encountered the flooding trigger directly (war story \\ref{ws-network-fabrics-microsoft-pfc-deadlock}).</p>\n<p><strong>Context</strong>: A team led by Chuanxiong Guo at Microsoft Research deployed RoCEv2 across Microsoft's data centers, a fleet operating at the scale of tens of thousands of servers, to support latency-sensitive services. Their design provided a lossless fabric with PFC, and the team developed a Differentiated Services Code Point (DSCP)-based PFC mechanism to extend the deployment beyond virtual local area network (VLAN) boundaries [@guo2016rdma].</p>\n<p><strong>Mechanism</strong>: PFC interacted with Ethernet flooding to create a cyclic buffer dependency. A dead server with an incomplete mapping between an IP address and a media-access-control address caused upstream switches to flood lossless packets to every port; PFC PAUSE frames then propagated upstream in response to the flooded traffic, forming a closed loop of paused buffers.</p>\n<p><strong>Impact</strong>: Lossless traffic through the switches in the cycle stopped entirely and stayed stopped, because no buffer in the dependency loop could drain without outside intervention.</p>\n<p><strong>Fix</strong>: Microsoft blocked broadcast and multicast traffic from entering lossless traffic classes, and dropped lossless packets when the corresponding Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) entry was incomplete rather than falling back to flooding.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems lesson</strong>: PFC backpressure is a structural vulnerability of lossless Ethernet at hyperscale. RoCE fabrics require packet-class discipline, PFC pause telemetry, and hardware-level safeguards because a mechanism designed to avoid packet loss can itself become the failure mode. ML training fabrics built on RoCE inherit this risk directly. A single incomplete ARP entry can freeze the lossless class that carries a multi-thousand-GPU training run's gradients, with the cluster appearing healthy while no gradient flows between workers.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-23\" class=\"cs-h4\">PFC storms and victim flows</h4>\n<p>In contrast to a static topological cycle, a <strong>PFC storm</strong> is a dynamic, runaway amplification phenomenon triggered when a single \"slow drainer\" (an accelerator stalled by memory bus saturation, operating system preemption, or a degraded transceiver with elevated bit error rates) fails to consume packets at wire speed. The attached ToR switch queue fills and sends a PAUSE frame to the sender. Because multi-port switches aggregate traffic from many sources across shared packet buffers, the paused queue blocks buffer allocation for unrelated packets. Packets of one priority class also leave the paused queue in order, so a packet bound for an uncongested port waits behind the blocked packets ahead of it (head-of-line blocking). The spine switches feeding that ToR then fill their buffers and pause the other leaf switches that send through them, and each additional tier widens the set of paused links until traffic between node pairs that never communicated with the stalled worker also stops. These victim flows turn one slow endpoint into a job-wide barrier delay, because every data-parallel worker whose path crosses a paused spine port now arrives late at the barrier.</p>\n<p>Congestion spreading is the mechanism behind both a storm and a deadlock. A storm is the form that a slow drainer sustains, so it can end when the drainer recovers, unlike the closed cycle of a deadlock.</p>\n<p>A degraded transceiver is one such slow drainer, so a component-exposure calculation shows how often a cluster should expect at least one, even though exposure alone does not estimate the probability of a PFC storm.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: A <code>[TransceiverDegradationRisk.n_gpus_str]</code>-GPU RoCE cluster, larger than the reference cluster, is in operation. If the probability that a given transceiver begins degrading on a given day is <code>[TransceiverDegradationRisk.p_degrade_pct_str]</code>, what is the chance that at least one transceiver degrades on a given day?</p>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>: Assume independent transceiver-degradation events with the same per-device probability.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Total exposure</strong>: <code>[TransceiverDegradationRisk.n_gpus_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[TransceiverDegradationRisk.links_per_gpu_str]</code> = <code>[TransceiverDegradationRisk.total_links_str]</code> links, or <code>[TransceiverDegradationRisk.total_transceivers_str]</code> transceivers.</li>\n<li><strong>Probability of zero transceiver degradations</strong>: <code>[TransceiverDegradationRisk.no_degradation_prob_math]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Probability of at least one degradation</strong>: <code>[TransceiverDegradationRisk.any_degradation_prob_math]</code> (about <code>[TransceiverDegradationRisk.any_degradation_prob_str]</code>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: Under these assumptions, a large RoCE cluster has a <code>[TransceiverDegradationRisk.any_degradation_prob_str]</code> daily chance of at least one degraded transceiver. This result motivates hardware telemetry and automated remediation, but estimating fabric-wide freeze risk additionally requires the conditional probability that a degradation triggers and propagates a PFC storm.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-23\" class=\"cs-h4\">Watchdog recovery and the lossy alternative</h4>\n<p>When a slow drainer or routing loop does stall a queue, switches can use <strong>PFC watchdog timers</strong> to detect prolonged pause states and trigger recovery without a full reboot. The configured threshold $\\PfcWatchdogTimeout$ must fit the deployment's pause and recovery budgets; a range such as $50\\text{--}200\\text{ ms}$ is a configuration example, not a universal requirement. Exceeding it indicates stalled progress, not proof of a cyclic deadlock. Depending on the implementation, mitigation can disable pause handling or discard affected traffic so blocked queues can drain. The transport must then recover any lost data, with the completion penalty determined by retransmission behavior, outstanding traffic, and the collective's dependencies. On an RNIC with Go-Back-N-style recovery (@sec-network-fabrics-losslessness), each discarded packet also costs the in-flight window behind it, so breaking a pause converts a stalled queue into a retransmission tail that the waiting collective absorbs.</p>\n<p>This systemic brittleness is driving hyperscale operators toward next-generation architectures such as the Ultra Ethernet Consortium (UEC) [@ultraEthernet2025specification], detailed in @sec-network-fabrics-protocol-convergence, and loss-tolerant RDMA designs [@mittal2018irn]. Designs that combine packet spraying (sending one flow's packets over many paths) with congestion control and selective retransmission can operate without link-layer PFC. Removing PFC removes its pause-based deadlocks and storms, but endpoint recovery must still bound the completion-time penalty of congestion and loss.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-23\" class=\"cs-h3\">End-to-end congestion control with DCQCN and HPCC</h3>\n<p>Because link-layer PFC fires only when a switch buffer is already near exhaustion, relying on pauses alone invites the cascades and watchdog drops of @sec-network-pfc-hazards. Keeping queues below those thresholds requires end-to-end congestion control, which throttles sender injection rates before buffers fill. For a BSP workload with a completion-time target, this rate control is a <em>latency requirement</em> rather than an <em>optional throughput optimization</em>: delaying a required transfer extends the synchronization boundary, so the controller must balance delivered throughput against queueing and recovery tails.</p>\n<p>The first widely deployed solution, <strong>DCQCN</strong>, operates as a reactive feedback loop using ECN [@zhu2015]. When a switch's queue depth exceeds a configured threshold, it marks the ECN bit in the packet header. The receiver echoes this mark back to the sender via a congestion notification packet (CNP), prompting the sender to reduce its injection rate using a multiplicative-decrease algorithm. DCQCN is widely supported, but production AI collectives expose a tuning problem because the feedback loop takes a full network round trip to take effect. Setting the ECN threshold high leaves too little buffer headroom for synchronized bursts to slow down before hitting the PFC pause threshold, whereas setting it low causes senders to throttle aggressively on brief queue spikes and underutilize the link. Consequently, ECN thresholds and NIC firmware behavior trade off PFC avoidance, throughput, visibility, and tail latency rather than offering one stable optimum [@gangidi2024rdma].</p>\n<p>To move beyond reactive threshold tuning, <strong>HPCC</strong> uses in-network telemetry (INT) to give the sender more information about path load [@li2019]. Switch measurements of queue occupancy and transmitted traffic let the controller estimate an appropriate injection rate rather than react only to a binary congestion mark. That information still arrives after a feedback delay, so the controller must account for changing load and avoid overreaction. The evaluated design reduces queue buildup and tail latency, but requires switch telemetry support and compatible endpoint processing. DCQCN needs less detailed switch feedback, trading that simpler deployment requirement against a different tuning problem.</p>\n<p>: <strong>DCQCN (data center quantized congestion notification)</strong>: Introduced by @zhu2015 (Microsoft and Mellanox) at SIGCOMM 2015 for large-scale RDMA fabrics, its binary ECN/CNP feedback loop is simpler to deploy than telemetry-rich schemes. However, it creates a delicate tuning surface for training collectives: Meta's 2024 RoCE deployment showed that aggressive ECN marking reduces PFC pauses but degrades collective completion time [@gangidi2024rdma].</p>\n<p>: <strong>HPCC (high precision congestion control)</strong>: Introduced by @li2019 (Alibaba) at SIGCOMM 2019, HPCC requires programmable switch ASICs that append INT metadata to packets at line rate, a hardware dependency that limits the fabrics able to deploy it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-24\" class=\"cs-h3\">Adaptive routing</h3>\n<p>Throttling senders with DCQCN or HPCC relieves a filling queue only by slowing the flows that cross it, even when parallel spine links sit idle. That imbalance is the ECMP hash-collision problem from @sec-network-workload-inversion: because static flow hashing, which works well for millions of small flows, pins each persistent elephant flow to a fixed path, an 8-flow AllReduce across 4 equal-cost paths can place 4 flows on one link while another carries none. In a synchronized training step, that overloaded link then determines completion time and can halve useful bandwidth.</p>\n<p>: <strong>ECMP (equal-cost multi-path)</strong>: ECMP selects a path by hashing on the 5-tuple (source/destination IP, source/destination port, protocol), which means the same flow always takes the same path. This determinism is a feature for packet ordering but a liability for ML. Because a single AllReduce ring produces a small number of persistent flows, hash collisions are not transient statistical events but hot spots that last as long as those flows do.</p>\n<p>Adaptive routing mitigates this problem by allowing switches to select among eligible output paths using congestion information rather than only a static hash. That choice moves traffic onto paths with room and adds no capacity, so it repairs a collision but cannot relieve a fabric whose paths are all full. The implementation is protocol- and device-dependent. Packet-level spraying can send packets of one connection over different paths, so the receiver may observe reordering. Compatible endpoint support must accept out-of-order placement or restore the transport's required ordering; the presence of InfiniBand alone does not guarantee arbitrary spraying across every path. Ethernet fabrics can also employ <strong>flowlet switching</strong>, which changes a flow's path between bursts separated by a gap large enough to preserve packet order across the candidate paths, so its usefulness depends on how often the workload leaves such gaps relative to path delay differences.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Packet spraying</strong>: Unlike standard Ethernet (which hashes flows to single paths to avoid reordering), packet spraying sends individual packets of a single flow across multiple paths. This can improve use of the available $\\BisectionBandwidth$ by AllReduce elephant flows when the transport can tolerate or repair reordering. The Ultra Ethernet Consortium (UEC) specification design explicitly includes multi-path packet spraying and flexible ordering to reduce the ECMP collision problem for AI/HPC traffic [@ultraEthernet2025specification].</p>\n<p>MoE expert exchanges illustrate why path diversity matters: a dense exchange among 64 expert-parallel ranks contains at most $64(64-1)=4{,}032$ directed remote rank pairs after excluding self-transfers, with active traffic governed by expert placement and token-routing sparsity. Where static-hash collisions concentrate that traffic onto a few links, adaptive routing spreads it across the provisioned $\\BisectionBandwidth$. Archetype B (DLRM at Scale) creates similar exchanges when batches of embedding requests and results move among table shards, although batch and shard placement determine the traffic matrix rather than every individual lookup requiring a global exchange.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-25\" class=\"cs-h3\">The incast problem in ML</h3>\n<p>Congestion control and adaptive routing manage flows that traverse the interior of the fabric. A different failure mode occurs at the edge, where the traffic pattern itself overwhelms a single port regardless of how much capacity the fabric interior provides.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Incast</strong></em> is a many-to-one ML cluster traffic pattern in which a large number of senders simultaneously transmit data to a single receiver port. It concentrates line-rate traffic from multiple sources into a single switch queue, and it can overflow the buffer if the burst outlasts the available buffering and control response, even when the rest of the fabric is uncongested.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: Flat aggregation, synchronized storage reads, or other fan-in phases can direct many senders toward one receiver. The deliberately worst-case scenario here assumes a RoCE fabric whose ports all run at <code>[IncastBurst.port_gbit_per_s_str]</code>. In it, <code>[IncastBurst.n_senders_str]</code> senders each transmit at <code>[IncastBurst.sender_bw_gb_per_s_str]</code> toward one switch egress, producing <code>[IncastBurst.offered_tb_per_s_str]</code> of offered load against a <code>[IncastBurst.port_gbit_per_s_str]</code> port. A <code>[IncastBurst.buffer_mb_str]</code> buffer then overflows in about <code>[IncastBurst.overflow_us_str]</code> under the stated assumptions, after which the switch either drops packets or, in a PFC deployment, pauses its upstream neighbors. The example is a stress bound, not the traffic matrix of a ring AllReduce.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Unlike general congestion (which occurs on shared internal links when aggregate traffic exceeds link capacity), incast is an endpoint bottleneck. It occurs even if every internal spine and leaf link is completely uncongested, because the bottleneck is the single destination port, not the fabric interior.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: Incast is not necessarily rare or unpredictable. Training jobs create it on a schedule, whenever many GPUs read dataset shards from or write checkpoints to the same storage node, and in the aggregation step of a tree or hierarchical reduction, where several ranks send partial sums to one parent. A gradient ring is different; its point-to-point flows can still collide at a shared egress, but that is a hot spot rather than many-to-one incast. The severity depends on the collective algorithm, topology, routing, and oversubscription, so it must be measured and mitigated rather than treated as an edge case.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In a worst-case incast, <code>[IncastCascadeFigure.offered_tb_per_s_str]</code> of offered traffic from <code>[IncastCascadeFigure.n_senders_str]</code> senders converges on a single <code>[IncastCascadeFigure.port_gbit_per_s_str]</code> (<code>[IncastCascadeFigure.port_gb_per_s_str]</code>) egress (@fig-congestion-cascade). Each sender injects at <code>[IncastCascadeFigure.sender_bw_gb_per_s_str]</code>. The buffer fills over a finite interval; control feedback can reduce arrivals, drops can discard excess traffic, or backpressure can move the queueing upstream. Whether that pressure spreads depends on shared queues and the paths carrying the traffic. Let the aggregate arrival rate to one egress be $\\EgressArrivalRate$, the egress service rate be $\\EgressServiceRate$, and the instantaneous queue occupancy be $\\EgressQueueOccupancy(\\TimeInstant)$. While a burst persists and $\\EgressArrivalRate &gt; \\EgressServiceRate$, the queue grows at the net rate</p>\n<p>$$ \\EgressQueueOccupancy(\\TimeInstant) = \\min\\!\\left(\\EgressBufferCapacity,\\; \\EgressQueueOccupancy(0) + (\\EgressArrivalRate-\\EgressServiceRate)\\TimeInstant\\right), $$</p>\n<p>where $\\EgressBufferCapacity$ is the finite buffer capacity. The ratio $\\EgressPortUtilization=\\EgressArrivalRate/\\EgressServiceRate$ distinguishes a drainable workload from sustained overload: for $\\EgressPortUtilization&lt;1$, idle intervals can drain the queue; as long-run $\\EgressPortUtilization$ approaches one, less slack remains to absorb bursts; and for $\\EgressPortUtilization&gt;1$, occupancy must reach a control threshold or the buffer limit if the overload persists. The queue in this model is a switch packet queue, distinct from the request queues of an inference server. Serialization and propagation set the unloaded path latency, whereas queued bytes add approximately $\\EgressQueueOccupancy/\\EgressServiceRate$ of waiting time.</p>\n<p>⇄ <strong>Contrast:</strong> Request queues, whose arrival and service rates set serving latency rather than switch buffer occupancy, are developed in @sec-appdx-inference-scale-queuing-theory-batched-inference-bbf0.</p>\n<p>In the deliberately worst-case incast above, $\\EgressPortUtilization$ is <code>[IncastBurst.oversubscription_mult_str]</code>, so the <code>[IncastBurst.buffer_mb_str]</code> buffer fills in about <code>[IncastBurst.overflow_us_str]</code>. Once full, that buffer represents <code>[IncastBurst.buffer_hold_us_str]</code> of egress waiting time. By contrast, the bandwidth-delay product (link rate times round-trip time) for the <code>[IncastBurst.port_gbit_per_s_str]</code> egress and an illustrative <code>[IncastBurst.feedback_rtt_us_str]</code> feedback round trip is only <code>[IncastBurst.bandwidth_delay_product_kb_str]</code>; it measures data in flight on the path, not the buffering needed to absorb an arbitrary fan-in burst. ECN must mark early enough for feedback to arrive before exhaustion, and in this worst case the buffer fills before a <code>[IncastBurst.feedback_rtt_us_str]</code> round trip completes, so even a mark on the first queued byte returns too late. PFC can prevent a drop, but its backpressure can spread congestion. A larger buffer extends the reaction window at the cost of a larger tail-latency bound.</p>\n<p>Synchronized ML traffic is susceptible to both true fan-in and shared-egress hot spots. When a layer finishes backward computation, many ranks can inject traffic at once, and flat or hierarchical aggregation phases can create true fan-in. Production clusters mitigate both cases along three complementary dimensions:</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Time.</strong> Layer-staggering triggers each gradient bucket's AllReduce as soon as backpropagation produces it. Final-layer gradients reduce while earlier layers are still computing, so communication is spread across the backward pass instead of released as one synchronized burst. The same staggering is communication-computation overlap, so it also hides transfer time behind the backward computation that remains.</li>\n<li><strong>Space.</strong> Collective algorithm selection bounds fan-in at individual switch ports. Hierarchical and rail-local reductions aggregate gradients in stages so that no single port must absorb all senders at once, and topology-aware placement keeps concurrent ring flows off oversubscribed ports.</li>\n<li><strong>Priority.</strong> Quality of service (QoS) classification tags gradient traffic with the highest service class to reduce interference from background storage or management traffic; shared links, buffers, and arbitration still constrain isolation.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>These mitigations spread the burst in time, space, and priority, but they do not eliminate congestion or tail latency. A production cluster must therefore deliver predictable step times with that residual congestion in place.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-26\" class=\"cs-h2\">Level 5 Cluster Design and Case Studies</h2>\n<p>Cluster design reduces to concrete engineering decisions: which cables to buy, how to wire the racks, which protocol to deploy, and how to validate that the resulting fabric delivers the bandwidth the training job expects. At this final level of the stack, wires, transport, topology, and congestion control combine into a coherent system, an end-to-end gradient bus that makes thousands of distributed GPUs feel like a single machine. Two representative large-scale architectures, one built on InfiniBand and one on Ethernet, illuminate the trade-offs that define ML infrastructure [@nvidia2023dgxSuperpodH100; @meta2024genaiInfrastructure; @gangidi2024rdma].</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-27\" class=\"cs-h3\">The GPU-to-GPU \"gradient bus\"</h3>\n<p>In a well-designed cluster, the network fabric acts as a scheduler-aware extension of the system bus, not a passive pipe. The ~<code>[NetworkFabricsClusterRecap.nvlink_to_ib_ratio_mult_str]</code> per-direction cliff between NVLink and one external port, stated at the start of this chapter, is the gap the cluster must hide. Communication-computation overlap, which the Fleet Law credits as $\\OverlappedTime$, is the first mechanism for hiding it, through the same layer-staggered AllReduce launch that spreads incast bursts in @sec-network-fabrics-incast. Overlap cannot hide the last mile of the exchange, because the gradients computed at the end of backpropagation have little computation left behind which to hide their transfer (@sec-communication-overlap).</p>\n<p>Placement is the second mechanism, because it decides which communication crosses each boundary. The placement that the bandwidth hierarchy of @sec-compute-bandwidth-hierarchy sets, tensor parallelism inside the NVLink domain with pipeline stages and data-parallel replicas spanning nodes, becomes a wiring rule at cluster scale, because each fabric tier must be provisioned for the traffic placed on it. The links between nodes must meet the pipeline's activation-transfer budget, which physical adjacency alone does not guarantee. The full fabric carries the data-parallel exchanges, which gradient accumulation makes less frequent and overlap partly hides. Production designs therefore differ mainly in how they provision that outermost tier, as the two that follow show.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-28\" class=\"cs-h3\">NVIDIA DGX SuperPOD architecture</h3>\n<p>The NVIDIA DGX SuperPOD, a concrete implementation of the gradient bus, provisions the outermost tier by obtaining losslessness from the link layer and aligning the wiring with the data-parallel ranks, so that the fabric's tail behavior depends on hardware mechanisms rather than on congestion tuning. It connects DGX H100 nodes using an NDR InfiniBand network [@nvidia2023dgxSuperpodH100]. Each node acts as a dense compute island, with <code>[IncastBurst.gpus_per_node_word_str]</code> H100 GPUs connected via NVSwitch, which gives each GPU <code>[NetworkFabricsClusterRecap.nvlink_h100_gb_per_s_str]</code> of bidirectional internal bandwidth. Externally, each GPU pairs with a ConnectX-7 NIC delivering <code>[NetworkFabricsClusterRecap.ib_ndr_gbit_per_s_str]</code> of injection bandwidth. Across a standard scalable unit of <code>[IncastBurst.su_nodes_str]</code> (<code>[IncastBurst.n_senders_gpus_str]</code>), this yields an aggregate injection bandwidth of <code>[IncastBurst.offered_tb_per_s_str]</code> (<code>[IncastBurst.n_senders_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[IncastBurst.sender_bw_gb_per_s_str]</code>), the rate the scalable unit can offer the fabric when every GPU transmits at once.</p>\n<p>This architecture connects all five levels of the network model into a single chain. At the wire (Level 1), it standardizes on <code>[NetworkFabricsClusterRecap.ib_ndr_gbit_per_s_str]</code> NDR links, choosing copper or optics by the reach between each node and its leaf and between leaf and spine. Because those links run InfiniBand's native link-layer credit-based flow control (Level 2), they provide a lossless medium without the pause-cascade fragility of Ethernet PFC. Above the transport, the topology (Level 3) arranges the switches as a rail-optimized fat-tree, aligning GPU rails across each scalable unit so that same-rail traffic stays close while cross-rail traffic traverses the spine layer (replacing the simpler per-rack ToR allocation of @sec-network-fabrics-tor, which illustrated switch radix). When cross-rank traffic climbs to the spine, hardware-based adaptive routing (Level 4) selects among spine paths to preserve bisection behavior. Finally, at the cluster level (Level 5), additional switching joins multiple SuperPOD scalable units into larger fleets [@nvidia2023dgxSuperpodH100]: for the 175B-parameter model, <code>[IncastBurst.su_per_ref_cluster_word_str]</code> SuperPOD scalable units wire together so that <code>[IncastBurst.ref_cluster_gpus_str]</code> function as one synchronous training system.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-29\" class=\"cs-h3\">Meta Grand Teton platform</h3>\n<p>Meta's Grand Teton clusters take the opposite approach, accepting Ethernet's congestion-tuning burden in exchange for multi-vendor supply and shared operations. Meta disclosed two Grand Teton-based <code>[GrandTetonClusters.n_gpus_value_str]</code>-H100 clusters: one using RoCE over an Arista/OCP Ethernet fabric and one using NVIDIA Quantum-2 InfiniBand, both with <code>[GrandTetonClusters.endpoint_gbit_per_s_str]</code> endpoints. Meta used both for Llama 3 training and reported ongoing Llama 3 training on the RoCE cluster [@meta2024genaiInfrastructure]. Ethernet lets Meta source switches from multiple vendors and reuse the optical infrastructure and management tooling of its front-end serving fleet, avoiding the operational silo of a dedicated InfiniBand island.</p>\n<p>Meta's RoCE study shows how Level 4 choices affect the complete fabric. It describes PFC watchdogs for long-duration pause events, iterative routing designs beyond plain ECMP, future-oriented flowlet switching experiments, and a 400G deployment in which DCQCN tuning proved difficult enough that Meta proceeded without DCQCN while relying on PFC plus higher-layer collective controls [@gangidi2024rdma]. That choice moved injection control from the NIC into the collective library rather than removing it, and the study cautions that the approach may not apply to all scenarios. The lesson is to validate the combination of endpoint behavior, routing, and flow control under the intended collective traffic. Large-transfer throughput alone does not establish small-message latency or recovery tails, and those results cannot be inferred from the Ethernet or InfiniBand label. Small-message latency matters in training through frequent small exchanges, which expose startup cost even when a full step lasts seconds, and in serving to a degree set by model partitioning, request shape, and service-level objectives (SLOs).</p>\n<p>These case studies close the five-level stack by showing how the physical wire, transport protocol, topology, and congestion controls become one production fabric. The remaining operational problem is not how to build a single fast cluster, but how to share that expensive substrate across teams and workloads without sacrificing the predictable performance that training demands.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-30\" class=\"cs-h2\">Network Virtualization</h2>\n<p>When a single training job leaves racks or spine links idle between runs, operators carve the cluster among multiple tenants and jobs, only to discover that a neighbor's checkpoint burst or evaluation sweep can starve a synchronous AllReduce across shared NICs and switch buffers. Network virtualization must make sharing compatible with bandwidth allocations, latency targets, and access isolation. These requirements interact because tenants still share physical links and buffers. An operator might reserve <code>[VirtualizationIdleCost.training_share_pct_str]</code> of the cluster's $\\BisectionBandwidth$ for training and allocate the remaining <code>[VirtualizationIdleCost.other_share_pct_str]</code> to inference and preprocessing. Enforcing that policy requires admission control and compatible scheduling along the relevant paths; a virtual NIC or separate traffic class alone does not guarantee the end-to-end share.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-31\" class=\"cs-h3\">Hardware NIC virtualization with SR-IOV</h3>\n<p>Routing multi-tenant traffic through a hypervisor's software virtual switch reintroduces the exact host-CPU overhead and microsecond jitter that RDMA was built to bypass. Cloud providers instead expose NIC resources to virtualized GPU instances through <strong>single-root I/O virtualization (SR-IOV)</strong>, a standard that allows a physical NIC to present multiple <strong>virtual functions (VFs)</strong>. Each VF exposes its own hardware queues, doorbell registers (which software writes to tell the NIC that work is waiting), and DMA mappings, and software can assign each VF to a workload. A directly assigned VF can remove host software mediation from the active DMA path, while drivers and virtualization software still configure access and manage the device. Performance depends on the NIC, I/O placement, and competing traffic. Because VFs share physical resources, placement logic must reserve NIC capacity and QoS policy alongside GPUs rather than treat the network as an unbounded pool.</p>\n<p>However, hardware-level isolation still needs explicit bandwidth policy. SR-IOV exposes multiple VFs that share the NIC's physical resources; administrators can add per-VF or per-group QoS policies to cap or guarantee bandwidth, such as assigning <code>[VirtualizationIdleCost.n_vfs_word_str]</code> VFs <code>[VirtualizationIdleCost.vf_rate_gbit_per_s_str]</code> each on a <code>[IbNdrVirtualizationRecap.ib_ndr_gbit_per_s_str]</code> NIC. For the 175B-parameter model training on a multi-tenant cluster, that configured partitioning can prevent a neighboring tenant from stealing bandwidth, but it also enforces a hard ceiling on peak throughput. The training job must be architected to operate within this slice, as no amount of software optimization can burst beyond the configured VF limit.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-32\" class=\"cs-h3\">Traffic isolation and quality of service</h3>\n<p>Per-VF rate caps bound what each tenant injects at the NIC, but traffic from different tenants still merges on the fabric's shared switch buffers and spine links. When the 175B model is midway through a gradient AllReduce and a neighboring job initiates a <code>[VirtualizationIdleCost.ckpt_gb_str]</code> checkpoint save, that burst occupies a <code>[IbNdrVirtualizationRecap.ib_ndr_gbit_per_s_str]</code> port for <code>[VirtualizationIdleCost.ckpt_s_str]</code> at full line rate. It can also claim a large share of the shared spine links for that interval, which stretches every AllReduce whose traffic crosses them. To prevent this noisy-neighbor effect inside the fabric, high-performance switches rely on <strong>quality of service (QoS)</strong> mechanisms that enforce fairness at the packet and queue level.</p>\n<p>InfiniBand isolates traffic through virtual lanes (VLs), which maintain separate credit and buffer state within a physical link across up to 16 virtual lanes (one of them reserved for subnet management) [@infiniband2000spec]. If a storage burst exhausts the credits of its own lane, a gradient lane with available credits can continue transmitting, even though both lanes still share link bandwidth and arbitration. Ethernet and RoCE fabrics achieve a similar separation through device-dependent <strong>traffic class</strong> mappings and queue policies. Within those mappings, <strong>enhanced transmission selection</strong> allocates a share of link bandwidth to each traffic class, and switches may also partition buffer and forwarding resources into <strong>network slices</strong>. Across both stacks, these mechanisms reduce interference only when classification, resource allocation, and scheduling agree along the entire path, because shared priorities or buffer pools can still couple tenants.</p>\n<p>Virtualization solves the sharing problem but makes performance diagnosis harder. When a training job slows down on a multi-tenant cluster, the cause could be a physical link degradation, a noisy neighbor exceeding its bandwidth allocation, or a misconfigured QoS policy. Distinguishing these cases requires systematic monitoring.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-33\" class=\"cs-h2\">Monitoring and Debugging</h2>\n<p>Network performance problems in ML clusters are insidious because they show up as <strong>silent waste</strong> rather than explicit failures. A degraded transceiver causing a <code>[SilentWaste.bw_loss_pct_str]</code> reduction in effective bandwidth might slow each training iteration by only <code>[SilentWaste.slowdown_pct_range_str]</code>. The slowdown is small because the lost bandwidth stretches only the exposed communication share of the step (the <code>[NonBlockingOversubscription.comm_pct_str]</code> assumed in the oversubscription estimate, less whatever overlap hides), and stretches it by roughly the inverse of the bandwidth that remains. A drift that small is easily masked by the natural variance of checkpointing or data loading. Over a <code>[SilentWaste.run_days_str]</code>-day training run on <code>[SilentWaste.n_gpus_str]</code>, this invisible drag accumulates to roughly <code>[SilentWaste.waste_gpu_hours_range_str]</code> wasted GPU-hours, burning about <code>[SilentWaste.waste_cost_range_str]</code> at <code>[SilentWaste.gpu_hour_rate_str]</code> without triggering a single alarm.</p>\n<p>Traditional IT monitoring tools like SNMP or ICMP ping measure connectivity, not the sustained throughput required by RDMA. Effective observability requires a three-layer approach: <strong>application monitoring</strong> (NCCL algorithmic bandwidth), <strong>transport monitoring</strong> (PFC pause frames, retransmission rates), and <strong>physical monitoring</strong> (FEC errors, signal attenuation) [@jeaugey2017nccl; @gangidi2024rdma]. The three layers retrace the five-level model from the top down. Application monitoring observes the collective that Level 5 integrates, transport monitoring observes the Level 2 recovery and Level 4 congestion machinery, and physical monitoring observes the Level 1 wire. Only by correlating signals across these layers can operators detect that a \"slow training run\" is caused by a single degraded cable in one rack.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-34\" class=\"cs-h3\">Link-level telemetry</h3>\n<p>A marginal cable or overheating transceiver rarely drops the link outright; instead, it accumulates symbol errors, triggers transmit discards, or retrains to a slower link state while the job keeps running. Catching that physical drift requires polling switch and NIC port counters, which report transmitted and received data, transmit discards, symbol errors, and link-down events; on InfiniBand a utility such as <code>perfquery</code> reads them, and Ethernet/RoCE devices expose the equivalent telemetry through their own management interfaces [@linuxManpages2026perfquery]. Data counters measure utilization, discards warrant investigation against the port's traffic and recovery policy, and symbol errors and link-down events are evidence of a degrading signal. Correlating those counters with link rate, lane state, and transport behavior narrows the cause; a counter alone does not identify a defective cable or transceiver.</p>\n<p>Polling link state alongside traffic counters can reveal <strong>silent degradation</strong>. A link operating at HDR (<code>[IbHdrReference.ib_hdr_gbit_per_s_str]</code>) rather than the expected NDR rate (<code>[IbNdrMonitoringRecap.ib_ndr_gbit_per_s_str]</code>) can remain connected while offering half the expected bandwidth. A link that trains with two of its four lanes active after a connector fault fails the same way: it stays up while its bandwidth is halved. Both cases are visible only when negotiated rate and lane state are compared with the deployment baseline.</p>\n<p>For <code>[SilentWaste.n_gpus_str]</code> and roughly <code>[SilentWaste.n_links_str]</code> links, the assumed <code>[SilentWaste.p_link_pct_str]</code> point-in-time degradation probability gives <code>[SilentWaste.expected_degraded_str]</code> degraded links in expectation and, under independent events, a <code>[SilentWaste.p_any_pct_str]</code> probability of at least one degradation. This probability measures something different from the daily figure in notebook \\ref{nbk-network-fabrics-probability-transceiver-degradation}. That notebook asks how often a new degradation begins; this estimate asks how many degraded links are present at any moment. Standing prevalence, the share of links degraded at any moment, is roughly the onset rate times how long a degraded link stays in service, and silent degradation, by definition, stays in service until someone looks, so prevalence can far exceed what a small daily onset rate suggests. Both rates are scenario assumptions rather than measured fleet data.</p>\n<p>A job slows when an affected path limits its required traffic; fleet-wide exposure alone does not determine the job's slowdown. When the degraded link lies on a ring AllReduce's path, however, the cost reaches every participant, because each round waits for its slowest hop and the whole ring proceeds at the pace of that one link.</p>\n<p>↪ Gray failure, the general case of a component that degrades without failing, and why a synchronous job feels it as a straggler, is developed in @sec-fault-tolerance-reliability-reliability-distinguishing-stragglers-failures-285d.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-35\" class=\"cs-h3\">Bandwidth and latency validation</h3>\n<p>Clean port counters and an active <code>[IbNdrMonitoringRecap.ib_ndr_gb_per_s_str]</code> NDR link state still do not guarantee that a node pair can deliver that rate to a training job: a degraded PCIe path, a cross-socket NUMA bounce, or a congested spine cut can throttle RDMA transfers without incrementing a single physical error counter. Active validation must therefore measure the bandwidth and latency the job can actually achieve across the complete host-to-host path. Pairwise tests such as <code>ib_write_bw</code> can populate an <strong>all-pairs bandwidth matrix</strong>, or a sampled subset for a large fleet [@linuxRdma2026perftest]. A low-throughput pair identifies a path to investigate. Comparing the affected pairs with routing and physical telemetry helps distinguish a shared switch or cable problem from endpoint limitations.</p>\n<p>For latency-sensitive synchronization, <code>ib_write_lat</code> runs a ping-pong test with small RDMA writes and reports half the measured round-trip time as one-way latency. A reading such as 2 $\\MicroUnitPrefix$s, 5 $\\MicroUnitPrefix$s, or 100 $\\MicroUnitPrefix$s must be compared with a baseline for the same endpoints, message size, path, and load, because no single value is a universal fault threshold or proof that traffic has fallen back to a slower transport. Application-level tests such as <code>nccl-tests</code> then exercise collective communication across the intended worker group. Point-to-point results establish a path baseline, while collective tests reveal costs from concurrent traffic and scheduling that isolated pairs cannot expose.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-36\" class=\"cs-h3\">Systematic debugging workflow</h3>\n<p>Combining those passive port counters and active benchmarks into a live triage workflow reverses the bottom-up order in which the tools were introduced. When a training job reports lower-than-expected throughput, the diagnostic order should preserve the layer model from the top down rather than starting with cables. The following sequence moves from application symptoms toward physical causes through the three monitoring layers, with steps 1 and 2 at the application layer, steps 3 and 4 at the transport layer, and step 5 at the physical layer:</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Check GPU utilization</strong>: Rule out compute bottlenecks with accelerator telemetry (<code>nvidia-smi</code>, for example). High SM utilization alone does not rule out a network bottleneck, because communication kernels can also keep SMs active. Correlate utilization with the compute and collective portions of the step timeline. Low utilization does not automatically implicate the fabric, however. A starved input pipeline (data loading or storage) produces the same symptom, so confirm the data path is keeping the accelerators fed before investigating the network.</li>\n<li><strong>Inspect collective-library logs</strong>: Enable the library's debug output (<code>NCCL_DEBUG=INFO</code> in NCCL, for example) to reveal which network transport was selected, the detected bandwidth between nodes, and any fallbacks to slower protocols.</li>\n<li><strong>Run point-to-point tests</strong>: Run a pairwise bandwidth test (such as <code>ib_write_bw</code>) between specific nodes in the job. A single degraded link can bottleneck the entire ring in a ring AllReduce.</li>\n<li><strong>Check PFC/ECN counters</strong>: Inspect switch counters along the path. Sustained PFC activity indicates persistent congestion that should be investigated at the scheduler or routing level.</li>\n<li><strong>Validate physical layer</strong>: Check symbol errors and CRC counts to identify failing transceivers or cables.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In this order, application symptoms and collective logs show whether the network is at fault and whether the library fell back to a slower protocol, point-to-point tests identify the failing path, transport counters show whether congestion is involved, and physical telemetry confirms whether the wire itself is degrading. Each layer therefore narrows the search before any hardware is replaced.</p>\n<p>Your <code>[NetworkRunningExampleCluster.params_str]</code> model training job has been running for <code>[DebugSlowdownScenario.run_days_str]</code> days on the <code>[NetworkRunningExampleCluster.n_gpus_value_str]</code>-GPU reference cluster. You notice that the iteration time has gradually increased from <code>[DebugSlowdownScenario.step_before_str]</code> to <code>[DebugSlowdownScenario.step_after_str]</code> (a <code>[DebugSlowdownScenario.slowdown_pct_str]</code> slowdown). The GPU utilization reported by <code>nvidia-smi</code> has dropped from <code>[DebugSlowdownScenario.util_before_pct_str]</code> to <code>[DebugSlowdownScenario.util_after_pct_str]</code>.</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>First diagnostic</strong>: choose the first diagnostic step, name the tool that confirms whether the bottleneck is communication-bound or compute-bound, and cite the evidence that distinguishes the two.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Physical layer</strong>: identify physical-layer causes that could explain a 50 percent bandwidth drop when <code>ib_write_bw</code> finds one node pair at <code>[DebugSlowdownScenario.degraded_bw_str]</code> instead of the expected <code>[DebugSlowdownScenario.expected_bw_str]</code>, and verify them using switch counters.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Collective impact</strong>: estimate the impact on overall AllReduce time when the degraded link lies in the ring AllReduce path, then evaluate whether a hierarchical reduction that routes less of the collective through that link would help.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"sec-37\" class=\"cs-h2\">Network Technologies That Move the Ceiling</h2>\n<p>Monitoring keeps deployed fabrics honest, but it cannot push past the physical ceilings it measures, such as the reach and power limits that copper approaches at high signaling rates. New fabric technologies earn attention only when they move one of those bounds. As clusters scale toward 100,000 GPUs, the durable design variables are power per bit, reach, and the ratio of compute to memory to network capacity, not the product names attached to a particular roadmap.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-38\" class=\"cs-h3\">Protocol convergence and link density</h3>\n<p>At that 100,000-GPU scale, the first ceiling to move is the transport and switching layer itself, because standard RoCEv2 leaves operators with fragile PFC pause thresholds and static ECMP hashing while InfiniBand locks the fabric into a single-vendor stack. The Ultra Ethernet Consortium (UEC)} addresses these reliability and load-balancing limits for AI/HPC traffic over Ethernet. Its Ultra Ethernet Transport design combines packet spraying across multiple paths, flexible ordering, multiple transport delivery services, congestion-control changes, and telemetry [@ultraEthernet2025specification]. The architectural question is how the selected service delivers the required completion and ordering semantics under congestion or loss. Packet trimming (cutting an overflowing packet down to its header instead of dropping it, so the receiver learns of the loss at once), retry, and endpoint recovery should therefore be judged by their effect on completion-time tails and resource cost. Whether the design reproduces a lossless-fabric mechanism is not the test.</p>\n<p>Alongside these transport changes, higher switch capacity attacks hop count as well as raw bandwidth. XDR InfiniBand pushes <code>[NextGenLinkDensity.xdr_port_gbit_per_s_str]</code> per port, and Ethernet roadmaps include 800GbE and 1.6TbE link speeds [@nvidia2026quantumX800; @ethernetAlliance2025roadmap]. Ethernet switch silicon with <code>[NextGenLinkDensity.eth_1_6t_port_tbit_per_s_str]</code> ports pushes aggregate capacity to <code>[NextGenLinkDensity.switch_capacity_tbit_per_s_str]</code> in current Tomahawk 6-class designs [@broadcom2025tomahawk6]. A single <code>[NextGenLinkDensity.eth_1_6t_port_tbit_per_s_str]</code> port delivers <code>[NextGenLinkDensity.port_ratio_str]</code> times the bandwidth of a 400G port, so the same ASIC can instead expose <code>[NextGenLinkDensity.port_ratio_str]</code> times as many 400G ports. Spending that capacity on radix rather than port rate is what flattens the topology, because fat-tree host count grows as $\\SwitchRadix^2/2$ at two tiers and $\\SwitchRadix^3/4$ at three. A cluster that previously required three tiers of switches may then be served by two when radix, port rate, and placement constraints line up, potentially reducing the number of optical links and switching stages. The transceiver savings require explicit port and cable accounting; removing one tier does not automatically halve the module count, and the latency benefit depends on the resulting paths and traffic.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-39\" class=\"cs-h3\">Optical interconnects</h3>\n<p>Pushing ports from 400 Gb/s to the 800 Gb/s and 1.6 Tb/s rates of @sec-network-fabrics-protocol-convergence takes more lanes, faster lanes, or both. Eight lanes in the 100 Gb/s class of current NDR links reach 800 Gb/s, but an 800 Gb/s port on four lanes or a 1.6 Tb/s port on eight lanes doubles the per-lane rate to the 200 Gb/s class, which sharply increases the insertion loss per meter of passive copper. As that usable reach shrinks, in line with the link-budget example in @sec-network-fabrics-serdes, the SerDes must spend more energy per bit on equalization to recover the signal. Active electrical cables, which add retimer chips to regenerate the signal mid-cable, extend that reach but add latency and power of their own, leaving optical interconnects as the primary lever on power per bit and reach.</p>\n<p>Co-packaged optics (CPO)} places optical engines close to the switch or accelerator on a shared package substrate. Moving the optical engine from the switch faceplate to the package shortens the SerDes-to-optics electrical path from centimeters of board trace to millimeters, which reduces the signal-conditioning burden before transmission over fiber. The receiving optical engine converts the signal back to electrical form. Fiber still introduces propagation delay and has a loss budget; packaging the optics closer to the processor does not remove those constraints.</p>\n<p>Co-packaged optics therefore changes the electrical portion of the link budget while retaining the reach advantages of optical transmission. Usable reach still depends on the fiber, connectors, optical power, and receiver design. The relevant comparison holds payload capacity and error targets fixed, then measures the power saved by the shorter electrical path against the packaging and maintenance costs of integration. The power side of that comparison can be estimated port by port for a single switch.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: How much optics power does a <code>[OpticalDividend.switch_capacity_tbit_per_s_str]</code> switch save by moving from pluggable transceivers to co-packaged optics?</p>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>:</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Pluggable architecture</strong>: <code>[OpticalDividend.n_ports_str]</code> ports $\\times$ <code>[OpticalDividend.p_pluggable_w_str]</code> = <code>[OpticalDividend.total_pluggable_kw_str]</code> for optics alone.</li>\n<li><strong>CPO architecture</strong>: <code>[OpticalDividend.n_ports_str]</code> engines $\\times$ <code>[OpticalDividend.p_cpo_w_str]</code> = <code>[OpticalDividend.total_cpo_kw_str]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Savings</strong>: The savings reach <code>[OpticalDividend.savings_kw_str]</code> of power per switch.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: Across <code>[OpticalDividend.n_switches_str]</code> switches, the assumed pluggable modules consume <code>[OpticalDividend.cluster_pluggable_mw_str]</code>. The modeled CPO configuration saves <code>[OpticalDividend.cluster_savings_mw_str]</code>, equivalent to the reference power budget of roughly <code>[OpticalDividend.equivalent_h100_gpus_str]</code> H100 GPUs. That equivalence measures the scale of the saving; using it for additional accelerators also requires compute capacity, power delivery, and cooling in the right locations. Optics power constrains both operating cost and the equipment a facility can support.</p>\n<p>The spine example for <code>[SpineOpticsCost.cluster_gpus_str]</code> assigns <code>[SpineOpticsCost.total_power_kw_str]</code> to pluggable optics. That subtotal counts only the selected spine-side endpoints. Applying the same fractional saving to those endpoints would require a CPO implementation with the assumed per-port power and capacity; opposite-end optics remain outside the comparison. Integration also changes serviceability: replacing a failed optical component may involve a larger assembly than swapping a pluggable module. The energy benefit must be evaluated alongside packaging, repair, and spare-capacity costs.</p>\n<p>For ML infrastructure, CPO could flatten the bandwidth hierarchy by narrowing the gap between intra-node and inter-node bandwidth. If inter-node links achieve bandwidth comparable to NVLink-class local fabrics (hundreds of GB/s per GPU), the bandwidth penalty for extending tensor parallelism beyond a single node would shrink. Cross-node tensor parallelism would then become economical for more workloads, provided that startup latency and collective frequency also fit the step-time budget.</p>\n<p>However, CPO introduces new challenges. Optical components are sensitive to temperature (laser wavelength shifts with temperature, requiring active thermal management), and integrating photonics on the same package as a 1,000 W GPU creates a hostile thermal environment for the optical components. The manufacturing processes for silicon photonics and CMOS transistors are similar but not identical, requiring separate fabrication steps that increase package cost and complexity. Hybrid integration, which places the photonics chiplet on a cooler region of the package substrate and thermally isolates it from the GPU die, is one way to manage that environment, at the cost of a longer electrical path than full co-location.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-40\" class=\"cs-h3\">Disaggregated and composable architectures</h3>\n<p>As the gap between package-level and rack-level interconnects narrows, architects can reconsider the fixed compute-to-memory ratio of the server node, which leaves compute or memory underused when workloads need different resource ratios. Compute Express Link (CXL) memory pooling allows supported hosts to access assigned memory capacity through a CXL path, while disaggregated designs make some resource allocations independent of the chassis boundary. This decoupling changes which links lie on the workload's critical path. Even so, disaggregation does not merge CXL memory access, NVLink communication, and InfiniBand transport into one interchangeable service; each path retains its own supported operations, bandwidth, and latency.</p>\n<p>↩ CXL memory pooling, which makes node memory capacity less rigid but remains a capacity tier rather than a high-bandwidth memory (HBM) replacement, is established in @sec-compute-cxl.</p>\n<p>A composed node therefore needs a new traffic budget. The $\\MessageStartupLatency$-$\\LinkBandwidth$ distinction between startup and transfer cost remains useful, but each path requires its own parameters and access model. Remote memory traffic can compete with other transfers, and fine-grained dependent accesses can expose latency that a bulk-bandwidth estimate hides. Disaggregation improves resource flexibility only when these added costs fit the workload's execution budget. Composition relocates the synchronization backbone onto new paths rather than eliminating it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-41\" class=\"cs-h2\">Fallacies and Pitfalls</h2>\n<p>Because every architecture from a copper rack to a composed pod remains bound by that synchronization backbone, judging an ML fabric by a single layer or by traditional data-center networking habits routinely stalls training and degrades cluster productivity. Seven fallacies and pitfalls capture the most common errors across the five-level stack.</p>\n<p><strong>Fallacy</strong>: <em>More bandwidth always means faster training.</em></p>\n<p>Engineers assume upgrading from HDR (<code>[IbBandwidthFallaciesRecap.ib_hdr_gbit_per_s_str]</code>) to NDR (<code>[IbBandwidthFallaciesRecap.ib_ndr_gbit_per_s_str]</code>) will yield proportional gains, but the $\\MessageStartupLatency$-$\\LinkBandwidth$ model (@sec-network-fabrics-performance-model) reveals this is only true in the bandwidth-dominated regime. For small models or the small-message phases of pipeline parallelism, the startup term $\\MessageStartupLatency$ (<code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.ib_alpha_us_str]</code> in this chapter's NDR scenario, including NIC processing, switch hops, and FEC) dictates performance. If a workload is latency-bound, a 2$\\times$ bandwidth increase may deliver only a small fraction of the line-rate gain while adding substantial power and transceiver cost.</p>\n<p>Consider a <code>[BandwidthFallacy.msg_kb_str]</code> message (typical for control synchronization). On <code>[BandwidthFallacy.hdr_bw_gbit_per_s_str]</code> InfiniBand, it takes <code>[BandwidthFallacy.t_hdr_us_str]</code>. Upgrading to <code>[BandwidthFallacy.ndr_bw_gbit_per_s_str]</code> reduces this to <code>[BandwidthFallacy.t_ndr_us_str]</code>, a <code>[BandwidthFallacy.speed_pct_str]</code> improvement despite doubling the link rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Fallacy</strong>: <em>Lossless Ethernet is as reliable as InfiniBand.</em></p>\n<p>RoCE over Ethernet can achieve throughput comparable to InfiniBand for large transfers, but lossless operation depends on the configuration and interaction of PFC, ECN, routing, buffers, and NIC behavior (@sec-network-fabrics-pfc). A slow drainer or firmware defect can trigger a PFC storm, where PAUSE frames spread beyond the original queue, and a forwarding loop or cyclic buffer dependency can freeze the lossless class outright. InfiniBand's per-hop credit mechanism avoids this specific reactive PAUSE-frame cascade, but the fabric still requires deadlock-free routing, sound virtual-lane configuration, and congestion management [@infiniband2000spec]. Both fabrics require validation and telemetry; RoCE adds particular sensitivity to the configuration of Ethernet losslessness. Procurement should therefore compare complete transport stacks rather than link rates, because the stack determines where flow-control state and operational risk reside even when nominal rates match.</p>\n<p><strong>Pitfall</strong>: <em>Accepting data-center oversubscription ratios for synchronous training.</em></p>\n<p>Oversubscription relies on the traffic crossing a shared boundary fitting its available capacity. Bulk-synchronous training can violate that assumption with coordinated bursts. In the cut-saturating scenario of @sec-network-fabrics-fat-tree, <code>[BisectionWasteRecap.oversub_ratio_b_value_str]</code>:1 oversubscription increases the modeled communication bound by <code>[BisectionWasteRecap.oversub_ratio_b_mult_str]</code>. Applied to a <code>[BisectionWasteRecap.cluster_cost_m_str]</code> cluster with the assumed <code>[BisectionWasteRecap.comm_fraction_pct_str]</code> communication fraction, the resulting idle-capacity estimate exceeds <code>[BisectionWasteRecap.waste_m_str]</code>. This is an equivalent-capacity comparison under the stated schedule, not an equipment loss or a universal penalty for every collective.</p>\n<p><strong>Fallacy</strong>: <em>TCP bandwidth tests are sufficient for ML fabric validation.</em></p>\n<p>Standard benchmarks like <code>iperf</code> measure kernel-based TCP/IP performance. TCP throughput depends on host processing, offloads, and tuning as well as wire speed; it has no fixed throughput ceiling independent of that configuration. An RDMA training job exercises a different data path, so a low TCP result does not establish that the RDMA path is slow, and a high TCP result does not validate collective performance. Validation protocols must use RDMA-specific tools from the <code>perftest</code> suite to match the workload's data path.</p>\n<p><strong>Pitfall</strong>: <em>Relying on adaptive routing instead of topology-aware placement.</em></p>\n<p>Adaptive routing can distribute traffic across available paths, but it cannot increase their total capacity. For a <code>[NetworkFatTreeRecap.n_gpus_str]</code>-GPU job spanning two oversubscribed spine groups, the group-to-group links become limiting when the collective sends more crossing traffic than those links can carry within its time budget. Placement can reduce that traffic, and routing can reduce path imbalance. Both must be evaluated against the actual collective schedule.</p>\n<p>↪ Topology-aware gang placement that keeps collective traffic off contended cross-rack paths is developed in @sec-fleet-orchestration-placement-algorithms.</p>\n<p><strong>Fallacy</strong>: <em>Network fabric problems always appear as hard failures.</em></p>\n<p>Network performance issues in ML clusters often show up as subtle training slowdowns rather than errors, because jobs continue to run while gradients arrive late. The silent-waste estimate in @sec-network-fabrics-monitoring shows how a small bandwidth loss accumulates into wasted GPU-hours over a long run without raising a single alarm. Detection therefore depends on comparing delivered throughput with a baseline, not on waiting for an error.</p>\n<p><strong>Pitfall</strong>: <em>Treating PFC and ECN counters as optional production details.</em></p>\n<p>Without discard counters and PFC pause durations, queue pressure and recovery activity can stay hidden behind a slow collective, so both belong in production telemetry, correlated with collective completion time and the affected paths. Rising values do not by themselves establish a failing transceiver or predict an inevitable job failure. Physical error counters, traffic load, routing, and configured watchdog actions help distinguish the causes and select a response.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-42\" class=\"cs-h2\">Summary</h2>\n<p>The five-level model of @fig-network-five-level-model bridges physical reach, transport primitives, switch topologies, congestion dynamics, and cluster integration into a single system contract. A fabric must provide both enough capacity for the workload's traffic and predictable completion at its synchronization boundaries. InfiniBand and RoCE distribute this responsibility differently across links, endpoints, and operational policy; the production cases show why neither port rate nor protocol name determines delivered performance.</p>\n<p>Three numerical anchors show how to apply the model. One GPU's NVLink bandwidth exceeds its own external port's per-direction bandwidth by <code>[NetworkFabricsContextScenario.nvlink_to_ib_ratio_mult_str]</code>; the modeled NDR path has equal startup and transmission costs near <code>[NetworkAlphaBeta.n_cross_ib_kb_str]</code>; and the <code>[BisectionDefinitionExample.oversub_ratio_str]</code>:1 oversubscribed cut increases the fixed-payload transfer bound by <code>[BisectionDefinitionExample.slowdown_mult_str]</code>. Each result depends on its stated configuration and traffic assumptions. In the Fleet Law, transfer time contributes to $\\CommunicationTime(\\DeviceCount)$ and residual synchronization waiting to $\\SynchronizationTime(\\DeviceCount)$; consistent timeline accounting prevents counting one delay in both. The communication and coordination axes of C³ meet where those dependencies determine useful compute progress.</p>\n<p>The same model guides design and diagnosis. Message sizes determine whether startup latency or bandwidth dominates; communication patterns determine which paths must carry that traffic. During operation, application throughput, transport counters, and physical telemetry distinguish inadequate capacity from contention or a degraded component. These measurements also test whether a proposed optical or composable architecture removes the limiting constraint or merely moves it.</p>\n<div class=\"cs-callout\"><div class=\"cs-callout-title\">Takeaways</div>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Synchronous training inverts data-center traffic</strong>: A bulk-synchronous step releases a few large, correlated flows that must complete before the next step can start, so the fabric is judged by collective completion time and its p99 tail rather than by average per-flow throughput. Static ECMP hashing turns flow collisions into persistent hot spots.</li>\n<li><strong>Link speed is not fabric speed</strong>: For traffic that crosses a fabric cut, available capacity bounds transfer time (principle \\ref{pri-bisection-bandwidth}). Workload placement and collective schedules determine how much data must cross, so advertised port speed alone cannot predict useful training throughput.</li>\n<li><strong>Latency and bandwidth limit different messages</strong>: The $\\MessageStartupLatency$-$\\LinkBandwidth$ model of @sec-network-fabrics-performance-model separates startup cost from per-byte transfer cost, with $\\LatencyBandwidthCrossoverSize = \\MessageStartupLatency\\cdot \\LinkBandwidth$ marking the regime change. Message-size distributions, not vendor peak numbers, determine whether to optimize software latency or hardware bandwidth.</li>\n<li><strong>Reliable delivery moves the risk</strong>: RDMA transports need reliable completion and bounded recovery, not one universal lossless-fabric mechanism. InfiniBand normally suppresses loss with link-level credits; many RoCE deployments use PFC and ECN, while PFC-free designs place more recovery responsibility at the endpoints. Each choice trades hardware flexibility against tail latency and operational complexity.</li>\n<li><strong>Topology must match traffic</strong>: Fat-trees buy flexible bisection bandwidth, rail-optimized designs give same-rank AllReduce traffic its own isolated switch network, and dragonfly or torus designs trade cabling and locality differently. The right fabric depends on whether the workload stresses AllReduce, AllToAll, or multi-tenant sharing, and optical circuit switching lets a pod revisit that choice per job at millisecond reconfiguration cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Losslessness and load create their own failures</strong>: PFC prevents drops by pausing senders, which can spread one slow drainer's congestion to victim flows or freeze a lossless class in a cyclic buffer dependency. Under synchronized load, end-to-end congestion control is a latency requirement, adaptive routing repairs hash collisions without adding capacity, and incast is spread in time, space, and priority rather than eliminated.</li>\n<li><strong>Telemetry saves GPU-hours</strong>: PFC counters, link error rates, bandwidth baselines, and application throughput must be correlated across layers. Without that observability, a degraded transceiver or PFC storm silently converts a high-end fleet into a queue of waiting accelerators.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>At fleet scale, the interconnect is part of the computer. Its value lies in how much useful computation it allows the participating accelerators to complete together. A fabric decision therefore has to be judged against the workload's communication pattern and synchronization schedule, because additional capacity helps only where those dependencies expose a communication limit.</p>\n<div class=\"cs-callout\"><div class=\"cs-callout-title\">Chapter Connection</div>\n<p>A fabric that completes every collective on time still leaves two flows of bytes unaccounted for. Each step needs its next input batch in place before the accelerators ask for it, and the fleet's training state must outlive the next hardware failure, which at this scale means writing multi-terabyte checkpoints on a schedule. Those checkpoint bursts are the traffic class this chapter isolated from the synchronization path, and for text pretraining they can outweigh the training corpus itself. @Sec-data-storage treats storage as the fleet's fuel line, sizing the tiers and pipelines that deliver inputs ahead of demand and persist model state without stalling the step.</p>\n</div>", "quizzes": [{"section": "The Synchronization Backbone", "type": "MCQ", "question": "On an H100 GPU server, why does moving gradient synchronization across node boundaries create a severe 'bandwidth cliff,' and by what factor does per-direction bandwidth drop when transitioning from local NVLink to a single NDR InfiniBand port?", "choices": ["Bandwidth drops by roughly $2\\times$ because optical conversion adds latency while maintaining similar wire transfer rates", "Bandwidth drops by roughly $50\\times$ because inter-node links must encapsulate packets in kernel TCP/IP stacks", "Bandwidth drops by roughly $9\\times$ (from $450\\text{ GB/s}$ per direction to $50\\text{ GB/s}$ per port) due to the physical distance and switching constraints of inter-node cables", "Bandwidth drops by roughly $100\\times$ because inter-node communication requires staging all gradients through CPU system RAM"], "answer": "The correct answer is C. On an H100 system, per-direction NVLink bandwidth is $450\\text{ GB/s}$ (half of its $900\\text{ GB/s}$ bidirectional total), whereas a single NDR InfiniBand port delivers $50\\text{ GB/s}$ ($400\\text{ Gb/s}$), representing an approximate $9\\times$ bandwidth cliff. The assertion of a $2\\times$ drop significantly underestimates the physical disparity between on-chassis copper traces and multi-meter inter-node cabling. The claim of a $50\\times$ drop mistakenly assumes inter-node traffic uses kernel TCP/IP rather than RDMA. The assertion of a $100\\times$ drop confuses GPUDirect RDMA transfers with legacy CPU bounce buffering."}, {"section": "The Synchronization Backbone", "type": "SHORT", "question": "In a 1,024-GPU synchronous training job on a 175B-parameter model, a degraded inter-node link carries a required collective transfer. Explain when its delay can leave the other participating GPUs waiting, and why the link fault alone does not establish a job-wide slowdown.", "choices": [], "answer": "If the degraded link delays the last required collective completion on the job's critical path, workers that finish their dependent work earlier wait at the synchronization boundary. The exposed delay depends on the participating group, routing, collective schedule, and overlap with independent computation; a fault on an unused path or a transfer whose delay remains hidden need not lengthen the step. The relevant evidence is a matched step and transfer timeline, not the existence of a degraded link elsewhere in the fleet."}, {"section": "How ML Networking Inverts Data-Center Assumptions", "type": "MCQ", "question": "Why can standard Equal-Cost Multi-Path (ECMP) routing produce persistent hot spots when a distributed ML training job has a small number of large gradient flows?", "choices": ["ECMP hashes each flow to one path, so several large gradient flows can collide on one link while other equal-cost paths remain underused", "ECMP introduces non-deterministic packet reordering that corrupts the internal state of GPU tensor cores", "ECMP relies on centralized SDN controllers that cannot update routing tables at microsecond timescales", "ECMP requires all switches in the fabric to maintain complete copies of the global model parameters"], "answer": "The correct answer is A. A large population of independent small flows can distribute load more evenly under hashing, but ECMP does not guarantee balance. With few long-lived gradient flows, several hashes can select the same path, and the imbalance can persist through the collective while other paths remain underused. The actual result depends on the flow population, hashes, traffic volumes, and provisioned paths. The statement regarding packet reordering corrupting GPU tensor cores misattributes network-layer reordering to hardware compute corruption. The option referencing slow centralized SDN controllers confuses distributed IP routing with SDN control planes. The claim about switch hardware holding model parameters confuses network routing state with accelerator memory."}, {"section": "How ML Networking Inverts Data-Center Assumptions", "type": "SHORT", "question": "Contrast how packet loss is handled in traditional web-scale TCP/IP networks versus RDMA-based ML fabrics (such as RoCEv2), and explain why packet loss in an ML cluster can cause a large performance drop.", "choices": [], "answer": "TCP with selective acknowledgments can retransmit missing regions. Reliable RDMA transports also recover loss, but the recovery granularity depends on the RNIC implementation. A Go-Back-N-style RNIC can retransmit data after the missing packet, extending the completion tail seen by synchronized workers; selective-recovery implementations can repair a smaller region. The performance penalty therefore depends on recovery behavior and the collective critical path, rather than following from RDMA alone."}, {"section": "Level 1 Wire and Link", "type": "MCQ", "question": "What is the primary physical trade-off introduced by adopting PAM4 (Pulse Amplitude Modulation 4-level) instead of NRZ (Non-Return-to-Zero) signaling in $400\\text{ Gb/s}$ and $800\\text{ Gb/s}$ network fabrics?", "choices": ["PAM4 triples the baud rate on the physical wire but eliminates the need for receiver equalization", "PAM4 doubles the data rate per symbol by using four voltage levels, but the reduced noise margin requires Forward Error Correction (FEC) with an implementation-dependent latency cost", "PAM4 replaces electrical copper signaling with optical coherent modulation, restricting maximum cable length to under $50\\text{ cm}$", "PAM4 eliminates link-level bit errors entirely, removing the need for physical layer error detection at the cost of doubling SerDes power consumption"], "answer": "The correct answer is B. PAM4 transmits 2 bits per symbol using 4 discrete voltage levels, doubling throughput without doubling the symbol rate. However, the voltage gap between adjacent levels shrinks by a factor of 3 compared to NRZ, requiring Reed-Solomon Forward Error Correction (RS-FEC) whose processing latency depends on the PHY and module implementation. The choice claiming PAM4 triples the baud rate while eliminating equalization is incorrect because PAM4 maintains the symbol rate while requiring more complex equalization. The option stating PAM4 replaces copper with optical coherent modulation confuses electrical modulation with optical transceivers. The assertion that PAM4 eliminates bit errors contradicts the physical reality that smaller noise margins increase raw bit error rates."}, {"section": "Level 1 Wire and Link", "type": "SHORT", "question": "Why can increasing per-lane signaling rate shorten passive Direct Attach Copper reach, and how should the resulting link budget constrain AI rack design?", "choices": [], "answer": "Higher signaling frequencies increase copper attenuation, consuming the available loss margin. Usable reach depends on lane rate, cable construction, connectors, and active components rather than aggregate port rate alone. Designers keep passive paths within the validated channel budget and consider active cables or optics when the required distance exceeds it; a rack boundary by itself does not determine the medium."}, {"section": "Level 2 Transport and the Performance Model", "type": "MCQ", "question": "How does GPUDirect RDMA optimize the end-to-end data path during a multi-node AllReduce gradient exchange compared to traditional host-staged network transfers?", "choices": ["It allows the network interface card (NIC) to read and write GPU High Bandwidth Memory directly over the PCIe bus without staging data through host CPU system RAM", "It compresses floating-point tensors in GPU L2 cache before writing them directly to the optical switch buffer", "It enables the GPU to execute TCP/IP checksum offloading in CUDA streaming multiprocessors", "It re-routes inter-node gradient traffic over host SATA storage buses to bypass network switch congestion"], "answer": "The correct answer is A. Traditional network transfers require copying gradient data from GPU memory to CPU system RAM, and then through the OS kernel buffer before reaching the NIC. GPUDirect RDMA enables peer-to-peer DMA over PCIe between the GPU memory and the NIC, eliminating intermediate host copies, reducing CPU utilization and host-staging overhead. The choice claiming tensor compression in GPU L2 cache describes a data compression technique rather than RDMA DMA bypass. The option regarding GPU-executed TCP/IP checksums is incorrect because GPUDirect RDMA offloads the data path to the NIC rather than executing transport checksums on GPU cores. The claim regarding SATA storage buses confuses storage interfaces with accelerator network interconnects."}, {"section": "Level 2 Transport and the Performance Model", "type": "MCQ", "question": "An engineer observes that upgrading network links from $200\\text{ Gb/s}$ (HDR) to $400\\text{ Gb/s}$ (NDR) reduces the transfer time of a $10\\text{ KB}$ pipeline control message by only about $11\\%$, whereas the same upgrade halves the transfer time of a $350\\text{ MB}$ gradient tensor. How does the $\\alpha$-$\\beta$ performance model explain this discrepancy?", "choices": ["Switch routing tables drop small packets more frequently than large packets due to flowlet timeouts", "High-bandwidth optical transceivers disable forward error correction for messages larger than $1\\text{ MB}$", "PCIe bus arbitration introduces dynamic power throttling on transfers smaller than $64\\text{ KB}$", "The $10\\text{ KB}$ message falls below the crossover point ($n^* = \\alpha\\beta \\approx 75\\text{ KB}$), where fixed startup latency $\\alpha$ dominates, while the $350\\text{ MB}$ message is in the bandwidth-dominated regime ($n \\gg n^*$)"], "answer": "The correct answer is D. In the Hockney $\\alpha$-$\\beta$ model ($T(n) = \\alpha + n/\\beta$), the crossover point $n^* = \\alpha\\beta$ marks the transition between regimes. For NDR InfiniBand ($\\alpha \\approx 1.5\\,\\mu\\text{s}$, $\\beta \\approx 50\\text{ GB/s}$), $n^* \\approx 75\\text{ KB}$. A $10\\text{ KB}$ message is in the latency-dominated regime ($n < n^*$), where fixed startup overhead $\\alpha$ (SerDes, FEC, switch hops) accounts for nearly all transfer time, so doubling $\\beta$ provides negligible benefit. A $350\\text{ MB}$ message is deeply bandwidth-dominated ($n \\gg n^*$), so transfer time scales inversely with $\\beta$. The option regarding switch routing tables dropping small packets confuses routing policies with transfer time physics. The claim about transceivers disabling FEC for large messages is factually false. The choice suggesting PCIe power throttling on small transfers misidentifies the source of the latency floor."}, {"section": "Level 3 Switch and Topology", "type": "MCQ", "question": "A cluster designer builds a three-tier non-blocking fat-tree network using radix-64 switches ($k = 64$). What is the maximum number of hosts this fabric can support at 1:1 bisection bandwidth subscription, and how many total switches are required across all tiers?", "choices": ["$2{,}048$ hosts and $128$ switches", "$16{,}384$ hosts and $1{,}024$ switches", "$65{,}536$ hosts and $5{,}120$ switches", "$262{,}144$ hosts and $20{,}480$ switches"], "answer": "The correct answer is C. For a $k$-ary three-tier non-blocking fat-tree, the maximum host count is given by $N_{\\text{hosts}} = k^3 / 4 = (64)^3 / 4 = 262{,}144 / 4 = 65{,}536$. The total switch count across core, aggregation, and edge tiers is $5k^2 / 4 = 5(4{,}096) / 4 = 5{,}120$ switches. The choice of 2,048 hosts matches a standalone two-tier fabric ($k^2/2$ hosts, which needs only $k + k/2 = 96$ switches), not a full three-tier fabric. The choices of 16,384 hosts and 262,144 hosts represent arithmetic miscalculations of the cubic Clos scaling formula."}, {"section": "Level 3 Switch and Topology", "type": "MCQ", "question": "Why does a rail-optimized network topology significantly reduce synchronization latency for 3D-parallel training jobs compared to a standard uniform fat-tree?", "choices": ["It replaces optical fiber cables with liquid-cooled copper buses across all rows", "It physically connects all GPUs of the same local rank (e.g., all GPU 0s) across server nodes to dedicated rail switches, allowing same-rail transfers within a leaf group to use a single switch hop", "It eliminates the need for intra-node NVLink by routing all tensor-parallel traffic through Top-of-Rack switches", "It dynamically routes all AllReduce packets through the GPU display engine to bypass PCIe contention"], "answer": "The correct answer is B. In 3D-parallel LLM training, data-parallel AllReduce occurs exclusively between GPUs holding the same model shard (identical local ranks). Rail-optimized topologies dedicate separate switch planes (rails) to each GPU rank index, allowing same-rank transfers within a leaf group to complete in a single switch traversal; larger rails and cross-group transfers still traverse additional switches. The option mentioning liquid-cooled copper buses confuses physical packaging with network topology. The choice claiming it eliminates intra-node NVLink is incorrect because a fast local fabric remains advantageous for intra-node tensor parallelism. The option proposing routing packets through the GPU display engine is technically absurd."}, {"section": "Level 4 Fabric Behavior, Congestion, and Routing", "type": "MCQ", "question": "In a RoCEv2 fabric, what is 'congestion spreading', which stalls victim flows, and what mechanism causes it?", "choices": ["Optical dispersion in fiber cables causing packet corruption across adjacent wavelength channels", "High ambient temperatures in switch chassis causing SerDes circuits to throttle line rates across all ports", "Software hypervisors duplicating tenant packets across multiple Virtual Functions", "Priority Flow Control (PFC) PAUSE frames propagating upstream from a single congested egress port, filling upstream switch buffers and pausing unrelated flows that share those intermediate switches"], "answer": "The correct answer is D. When a switch port experiences buffer buildup (e.g., from an incast burst), PFC emits PAUSE frames upstream to prevent packet loss. As upstream buffers fill with paused traffic, those switches in turn send PAUSE frames to their upstream neighbors. This backpressure cascade can propagate across multiple switch tiers, blocking unrelated traffic (victim flows) that happens to traverse the paused intermediate switches. The option citing optical dispersion confuses physical-layer fiber physics with link-layer flow control. The choice attributing slowdown to thermal SerDes throttling describes a hardware thermal response rather than network congestion. The option describing hypervisor packet duplication confuses virtualization routing with link backpressure."}, {"section": "Level 4 Fabric Behavior, Congestion, and Routing", "type": "MCQ", "question": "How does High Precision Congestion Control (HPCC) achieve faster convergence and lower queue buildup during incast bursts compared to DCQCN?", "choices": ["HPCC drops all out-of-order packets at the edge switch and forces senders to switch to TCP cubic", "HPCC uses In-Network Telemetry (INT) to append precise switch queue depth, link utilization, and timestamps to packet headers, allowing senders to estimate available capacity and adjust rates rapidly", "HPCC disables Priority Flow Control and relies exclusively on application-level checkpointing to tolerate packet loss", "HPCC increases switch buffer sizes to $10\\text{ GB}$ per port to eliminate the possibility of buffer overflows"], "answer": "The correct answer is B. DCQCN relies on coarse, binary Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) marking and reactive Congestion Notification Packets (CNP), requiring multiple RTTs to converge via heuristic multiplicative decrease. HPCC leverages In-Network Telemetry (INT) where programmable switch ASICs embed precise metadata (exact queue depth, link load, timestamps) directly into packet headers, enabling senders to estimate available capacity and adjust rates rapidly rather than infer congestion from binary marks alone. The choice stating HPCC drops packets and falls back to TCP cubic contradicts RDMA design principles. The option claiming PFC is disabled with reliance on checkpointing misrepresents congestion control. The option suggesting $10\\text{ GB}$ switch buffers is economically and physically infeasible on high-radix switch silicon."}]};
